export const WATER_APPLIES_MD: string = "# Applies cards — VWBA 2.0\n\n**For Phoebe (beta) — Eligibility and Feasibility specialist. Pack `vwba-2.0`.**\n\n**Status: approved by the maintainer, 23 Sep 2026.** Drafted at the repository root on 22 Sep\n2026, its \"If the answer is no\" sections redrafted under the guide-not-gate rule on 23 Sep, graded\n\"passes as redrafted\", and moved here whole on her word, beside the six criteria, the ten\nconsiderations and the nine routes. Item K7, eyeball stop 1. **Nothing reads this file yet**: the\npack-keyed reader and gate come at the K7 proposal's step 5.\n\nTwo cards. Together they are the **\"does this apply\" test** for the water pack: they say\nwhether the volumetric-water-benefit pathway is in play for the project in front of\nPhoebe. **They never say eligible.** A pathway that applies still has all six criteria to\nmeet. All wording is a rewrite; no sentence is copied from the source. The source PDF stays\nin `sources-local/`, which is gitignored and never publishes; this file cites and links it.\n\n---\n\n## How the test is run, and what it is not\n\n**Two yes-or-no questions.** Both yes and the water pathway applies: the six-criteria\nsection of the worksheet opens, exactly as it does today. A no is sorted before anything is\nruled out, under the guide-not-gate rule of 23 Sep 2026: each card's \"If the answer is no\"\nsays which kind it is — Fixable, with a cited route from `routes-cards-vwba.md`; Unknown, not\na fail; or Blocked, where no cited route exists — and Phoebe says so once and goes on with\nwhatever other pathway is in play.\n\n**The record first.** What the visitor told Wellington in \"what it does\" usually settles\nW1 without a question, and often W2. Phoebe asks only for what those words leave open.\n\n**Questions cover both pathways where one answer can.** Maintainer's ruling of 22 Sep 2026\n(R4). \"What does the project do, in a sentence?\" answers W1 here and T1 on the carbon pack\nat once. A verdict covers every card and row the answer settles.\n\n**Why there is no version card.** One version of the guidebook is cited and it carries no\ntransition rule; nothing sorts a project by when it began.\n\n**Not a verdict, not a count.** The two cards carry no state beyond *applies* or *does not\napply* for the pathway.\n\n---\n\n## Source being cited\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Document** | *Volumetric Water Benefit Accounting 2.0: Guidance for implementing, evaluating, and claiming volumetric water benefits of water stewardship projects* (Guidebook) |\n| **Authors / publisher** | World Resources Institute, LimnoTech, Bluerisk, Bonneville Environmental Foundation. Washington, DC: World Resources Institute |\n| **Version and date** | Version 1, September 2025 |\n| **DOI** | https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112 |\n| **Sections cited** | Glossary, pp. 65–66; Appendix A, criterion 1, p. 32; Appendix C, Table C-1, pp. 37–39; Step 3, p. 21 |\n| **Local copy** | `sources-local/Methodology/vwba-2.0/VWBA_V2.0_Volumetric-Water-Benefit-Accounting-2.0-Guidebook.pdf` — 72 pages; printed page numbers match PDF page numbers 1:1, confirmed against the page footers |\n\n---\n\n## Card W1 — A water stewardship activity with a volume behind it\n\n**The test in plain words.** The project is a water stewardship activity: work done in a\ncatchment, outside the sponsor's own walls, that either changes how water moves through\nthat catchment for the better, or helps ease a water problem the people and bodies there\nshare, or both. And the good it does can be put as a volume of water over a period of\ntime. That volume is what the guidebook calls a volumetric water benefit, and the whole\npathway rests on there being one to count. Work that does neither of those things, or\nwhose good cannot be put as a volume, is not this pathway, however worthwhile it is.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Glossary, \"volumetric water benefits\n(VWBs)\" and \"water stewardship\", p. 66; Appendix A, criterion 1 — \"Established pathway for\na quantifiable VWB\", p. 32.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for\nthis guidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** Every project asking about this pathway.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- What the activity does on the ground, in a sentence.\n- Which shared water problem it takes on, or how it changes the water picture, in the\n  owner's words.\n- Whether anyone expects to state the result as a volume of water, and roughly how.\n\n**If the answer is no.** Phoebe sorts the no before she says anything is out. *Fixable* when\nthe project does something for the water but nobody has named the shared challenge or the\nvolume: route R-2 finds the challenge from the catchment, route R-3 picks the indicator and method,\nand route R-1 reworks the scope with the implementer. *Blocked* on the water pathway only when the\nactivity does nothing to the water of any catchment at all; no cited route changes that, and the\ncarbon pathway may still apply, so she goes on with T1. *Unknown* when the visitor cannot yet say\nwhat the activity does for the water: not a fail; she asks once and leaves it Unknown.\n\n---\n\n## Card W2 — A mapped route to counting it\n\n**The test in plain words.** There is a way to work the volume out. The guidebook keeps a\ntable of the most common project activities, and for each one it names what to measure and\nwhich method counts it. If the project's activity is in that table, the route is mapped.\nA project that gives households or a community access to drinking water sits in the\ntable's water, sanitation and hygiene row and maps to the Volume Provided method, D-3.\nIf the activity is not in the table, it is not thereby ruled out: the route has to be\nestablished some other way, with a method resting on the same principles, and that is a\nquestion for criterion 1 rather than a reason to stop here.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix C, \"Guidance for indicator and\nmethod selection\", Table C-1, pp. 37–39, the WASH row; Step 3, p. 21, which sends a\npractitioner to that table; Appendix A, criterion 1, p. 32.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for\nthis guidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** Every project asking about this pathway.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The kind of activity, in words the table would recognise: new water supply, treatment,\n  leak repair, recharge, restoration, and so on.\n- Where they expect the number to come from, if they already know.\n- For an activity not in the table: what method they would use and why it holds to the\n  same principles.\n\n**If the answer is no.** Never *Blocked*: the guidebook says an activity outside its table is\nnot ruled out (Appendix C, p. 39). *Fixable* when the activity is in the table but the route has\nnot been chosen: route R-3, and route R-8 for a drinking-water project. *Unknown* when the\nactivity is outside the table and no method has been named yet: not a fail. She says the route\nhas to be established under criterion 1 with a method resting on the same principles, points at\nthe subject matter expert the guidebook names for that choice (Step 3, p. 21), and offers the save\ndoor.\n";

export const WATER_ELIGIBILITY_MD: string = "# Eligibility rule cards — VWBA 2.0, Appendix A\n\n**For Phoebe (beta) — Eligibility and Feasibility specialist.**\n\n**Status: approved by the maintainer, 21 Aug 2026.**\n\nSix cards, one per eligibility criterion. All wording is a rewrite. No sentence is copied from\nthe source. The source PDF stays in `sources-local/`, which is gitignored and never publishes —\nthis file cites and links it, and does not reproduce it.\n\n---\n\n## Design decision — a guide, not a gate\n\n**Maintainer's ruling, 23 Sep 2026.** It replaces the ruling of 20 Aug 2026, whose wording is in\nthis pack's CHANGELOG under 0.4.5. All six criteria still have to be met for a project to generate\na benefit; what changed is what a miss means. A criterion not met today is one of three things:\nfixable, with a cited route from the guidebook or a real published project; unknown, with what to\nfind out; or truly blocked, and only the cards say which criteria can ever be that. The answer for\nthe pathway is a readiness read, likely eligible, likely not, or not enough known yet, never pass\nor fail. There is still no weighting, no partial credit and no averaging across criteria; a\nreadiness read is a sort, not a score. The rule itself has one home, *Pace and posture* in\nAGENT_RULES.md; the worksheet carries the three kinds of miss and the read once item A18 builds.\n**Until then the worksheet has its three states, and a criterion not met is \"Not yet\" with a route\nforward**, which was always the posture.\n\n**Every card carries a Phase line**, the maintainer's ruling of 23 Sep 2026: the phase on the\nnavigation where the row is acted on — Eligibility, Partners, Quantify, Plan, Monitor,\nCommunicate — or *To remain eligible*. **Eligibility-phase rows are the free screen**: facts a\nplanner can answer from the idea itself, whose answer can end a pathway or is needed to know it\napplies. On this site Phoebe asks those rows only, and the readiness read is on them. Every other\nrow is shown once, grouped by phase with the seat that helps there, never asked, never a mark\nagainst a planned project; the paid site walks every row. Here: criteria 1, 2 and 4 are\nEligibility; 3 and 6 Partners; 5 Plan, with the part kept up in Monitor.\n\n**Every \"not yet\" has to arrive with a route forward.** A verdict that only reports failure is not\nan acceptable output. Whenever Phoebe finds a criterion unmet, she states what would change that:\nthe specific evidence, document, consultation, or design change that would move the project from\n*not yet* to *met*. The posture is the rule.\n\n**Figure 3 is not only absolute.** It loops a project that misses back to the implementer to\nrevisit scope and re-evaluate, and only the second miss ends in \"not eligible\" (Step 2.3,\nFigure 3, p. 19). The build follows the whole figure, loop included.\n\n## Naming — where this card set sits\n\n| Set | Source | Role | State |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Eligibility** | Appendix A | The hard gate. Six absolute criteria; failing any one means not eligible. | This file |\n| **Feasibility** | Appendix B | Selection considerations for explorers — helps rank and choose among projects that already pass the gate. Never a bar to entry. | ~~Planned, **not drafted**~~ **Drafted, approved and live** — [feasibility-cards-vwba.md](./feasibility-cards-vwba.md) |\n\nAppendix B's considerations are explicitly not eligibility requirements (pp. 20, 34), which is why\nthey are a separate set rather than more cards here. ~~Not to be drafted until asked.~~\n\n---\n\n## Source being cited\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Document** | *Volumetric Water Benefit Accounting 2.0: Guidance for implementing, evaluating, and claiming volumetric water benefits of water stewardship projects* (Guidebook) |\n| **Authors / publisher** | World Resources Institute, LimnoTech, Bluerisk, Bonneville Environmental Foundation. Washington, DC: World Resources Institute |\n| **Version and date** | Version 1, September 2025 |\n| **DOI** | https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112 |\n| **Section cited** | Appendix A, \"Project eligibility criteria\" |\n| **Pages** | 32–33 |\n| **Local copy** | `sources-local/Methodology/vwba-2.0/VWBA_V2.0_Volumetric-Water-Benefit-Accounting-2.0-Guidebook.pdf` — 72 pages; printed page numbers match PDF page numbers 1:1, confirmed against the page footers |\n\nCross-references used for context, cited on the cards where they apply:\n\n- **Step 2.3, p. 19** — the project selection decision framework, including Figure 3.\n- **Step 2.3 summary box, p. 20** — a short restatement of all six criteria.\n\n---\n\n## How these six fit together\n\nAll six have to be satisfied. Figure 3 on p. 19 routes a project that falls short of any one of\nthem out of eligibility, with an intermediate option: go back to the implementer, see whether the\nscope can be reworked, and assess it again. Only a project clearing all six is eligible to\ngenerate a volumetric water benefit.\n\nThese criteria govern **whether a project can generate a benefit at all** — not what a sponsor is\nthen allowed to say publicly about it. Appendix A sets the claim-making question aside deliberately\n(p. 32). The pointer to where that question *is* handled — Step 6 — comes from the summary box on\np. 20, not from Appendix A itself.\n\nAppendix A also frames what the six are collectively meant to screen for (p. 32): benefits resting\non sound and consistent calculation, grounded in a real local context and going beyond what would\nhave happened anyway, not helping one party at another's expense, not producing unintended harm to\nthose depending on the water, and still checkable in later years for the life of the claims.\n\n---\n\n## Card 1 — A working route to a countable benefit\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The project has to change the water picture for the better — either by\naltering how water moves through the catchment, or by easing a shared water problem, or both. That\nimprovement then has to be countable as a volume. The count comes from comparing the situation with\nthe project against the situation without it, worked out either with a VWBA method or with another\nmethod resting on the same principles.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** The criterion asks for a route, and a route is a thing that can be chosen\nafter the fact: the activity table maps the common activities to an indicator and a method, and an\nactivity outside the table can be counted by a method resting on the same principles. A project\nwith no route yet has work to do, not a wall. Routes: R-3, R-8; for a project whose scope needs\nchanging first, R-1. (Appendix A, criterion 1, p. 32; Step 3, p. 21.)\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix A, criterion 1 — \"Established pathway for\na quantifiable VWB\", p. 32. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The objective of the activity, stated.\n- Confirmation the activity works on a shared water challenge.\n- The indicator chosen to measure the benefit.\n- The calculation method named — and where it is not a VWBA method, the reasoning for why it holds\n  to the same principles.\n\n---\n\n## Card 2 — The problem is real in that specific place\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Whatever water problem the project takes on has to be one that actually\nexists in that catchment or area. It should also be documented or well-understood locally — at\ncommunity, basin, or regional level — and it has to matter to the people and bodies who depend on\nthat water, including communities, agencies and tribes.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Whether the water problem is real and documented is a matter of looking:\nthe guidebook's first step is understanding the catchment and finding the shared challenges, by\ndesktop research or by talking to the people who depend on the water. A project that has not yet\nnamed its challenge has not yet done Step 1. Route: R-2. (Appendix A, criterion 2, p. 32; Step 1,\npp. 16–17.)\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix A, criterion 2 — \"Water challenges\naddressed relevant to the catchment or area of interest\", p. 32. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The project site, mapped.\n- How the local water challenges were identified — desktop research, or engagement with the\n  community and others relying on or advocating for the water.\n- An explicit link from the project's objective and activity to one of those identified challenges.\n\n---\n\n## Card 3 — Support from inside and from the neighbours\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Two audiences have to be behind the project. Inside the sponsoring\norganisation there needs to be genuine buy-in. Outside it, the groups connected to that water —\ncommunities, agencies, Indigenous peoples, and others who rely on it or speak for it — need to be\nbroadly supportive of the hydrologic benefit being proposed. How deep the consultation goes depends\non local conditions, and can be run by implementing partners or others with local knowledge. A\nhigh-risk project, or one in a place where reputation is delicate, deserves more of it.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Buy-in inside the sponsor and support outside it are earned by\nconsultation, and the criterion's own evidence list is a list of things a project can go and do.\nHow much is needed scales with the risk of the place. Routes: R-4, R-9. (Appendix A, criterion 3,\np. 33.)\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix A, criterion 3 — \"Internal buy-in and\ngeneral support from external water resources entities\", p. 33. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n**This list is depth of proof. It is not a second set of gates.** There is one criterion on this\ncard — both audiences are behind the project — and the four items below are how deeply that single\nthing is evidenced, not four further tests a project has to pass one by one. **How much is asked\nfor scales with local conditions**, exactly as the rule above says: a high-risk project, or one in a\nplace where reputation is delicate, is asked for more of it. A project carrying less of this has\nthinner proof of the same one thing; it has not failed four separate checks.\n\n**Phoebe must never present these four as four gates**, must never count them, and must never say a\nproject met three of four. The criterion is met or it is not yet met, and where it is not yet met\nthe route forward names the evidence that would carry it — which is what these four describe.\n\n- A community consultation, or desktop evidence gathered before the project began, establishing\n  that it matters to others.\n- Any concerns surfaced, along with thinking about what they imply — who gains, and which values\n  the project serves.\n- Evidence of working with implementers to hold trade-offs down.\n- The reasoning behind the decision to back the project, communicated to interested parties.\n\n---\n\n## Card 4 — The benefit is genuinely additional\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The project has to produce a gain, or head off a harm, that would not\nhave occurred otherwise. Work a sponsor is legally obliged to carry out for compliance does not\nqualify. Two situations sit outside that bar. First, where a sponsor is legally required to fund\nenvironmental work through a wider corporate social responsibility obligation, that money may go to\na project generating a benefit, provided the additionality and the intent are both present. Second,\nwhere the legal duty sits with the site owner but that owner has neither the capacity nor the\nengagement to deliver a real improvement, a sponsor who steps in may count the resulting benefit.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on one fact: whether the sponsor is legally obliged to do this work.**\nWork the sponsor must do by law to comply cannot be made additional by any evidence, and this is the\none water criterion that can end Blocked. But the criterion names two situations that are outside\nthat bar: money a sponsor must spend under a wider corporate social responsibility duty may fund a\nproject that generates a benefit, if the additionality and the intent are both there; and where the\nlegal duty sits with a site owner who cannot or will not deliver, a sponsor who steps in may count\nthe benefit. Phoebe asks the one fact first; Blocked only when the visitor says the work is the\nsponsor's own legal duty and neither situation applies. Route for the two situations: R-5.\n(Appendix A, criterion 4, p. 33.)\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix A, criterion 4 — \"Change delivered beyond\nthe without-project conditions (change that would not have happened without the activity)\", p. 33.\nShort restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Confirmation that the sponsor is not under a legal requirement to do this work.\n- Where the site owner *is* under such a requirement, written reasons why compliance would not\n  happen without the sponsor's support — or why the activity reaches past what the law demands.\n\n---\n\n## Card 5 — A route to keep checking the volumes\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The design has to include, from the outset, a way of keeping track of\nthe project and reporting on it once the work is done. That means arrangements that persist —\nenough to keep confirming the project still does what it was built to do, for as long as the\nbenefit is being claimed, or across the project's whole lifetime if that is what the sponsor wants.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** A tracking and reporting plan is made, not found: the guidebook says how,\nin Appendix E, and says to make it at selection or contracting time, with the implementer, and to\nwrite the money and the people for it into the agreement. Routes: R-6, R-9. (Appendix A, criterion\n5, p. 33; Appendix E, pp. 62–64; Step 4.3, p. 25.)\n\n**Phase.** Plan. Kept up by: running it for as long as the benefit is claimed (Appendix E, pp. 62–64), which for a planned project shows in the preview block and is settled by consideration B-2.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix A, criterion 5 — \"Established pathway to\ntrack project volumetric outputs\", p. 33. Short restatement at p. 20. Appendix E carries the\ndetailed guidance on building such a plan, beginning at p. 62.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- A tracking and reporting plan, drawn up with the implementer at selection or contracting time —\n  not after the fact.\n- The outputs and outcomes it will track, clearly identified.\n- Evidence the capacity and resources exist to run it, in money and in people.\n\n---\n\n## Card 6 — The downsides have been looked for and cut back\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Someone has to have gone looking for the ways this project could hurt\nsomebody or backfire, understood what they found, and acted to reduce it. That review can happen\nbefore implementation, after it, or both. The guidance offers examples of the kind of thing to look\nfor: farmers harvesting less after changing practice, vegetation thinning once invasive species are\npulled out, a stream's base flow dropping where reclaimed wastewater is used, and water access\nprojects that serve one party at another's cost.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Looking for trade-offs is work that can be done before the project\nstarts, and the criterion's evidence list says what it looks like: a desktop review, consultation,\ntechnical evidence, closer attention where the risk is high, and the reasoning communicated. A very\nlarge project gets latitude on how exhaustively it maps them, never on whether it looked. Route:\nR-7. (Appendix A, criterion 6, p. 33.)\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix A, criterion 6 — \"Trade-offs assessed,\nunderstood, and minimized\", p. 33. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- A desktop review, consultation with others, and/or technical evidence — gathered before the\n  project started — identifying trade-offs and what they imply. How deep this goes scales with\n  local conditions and the size of the project.\n- Closer attention where the risk of adverse outcomes is high, or the region is reputationally\n  sensitive.\n- For large-scale work with complicated trade-offs: evidence they were assessed *and* communicated\n  to the relevant parties before the activity began.\n- Evidence of working with implementers to minimise what was found.\n- The justification for supporting the project, communicated to interested parties.\n- Note that the guidance allows extra latitude for transformational, very large-scale projects\n  where mapping every trade-off is impractical or not feasible.\n\n**The latitude does not open the gate.** Figure 3 (p. 19) is absolute, and the maintainer's ruling\nof 20 Aug 2026 is that the build follows Figure 3 — so **criterion 6 is a hard gate for a\ntransformational project exactly as it is for any other.** What the latitude changes is **how\nexhaustively trade-offs must be mapped** where mapping every one of them is impractical. It does not\nchange whether they were looked for, understood, and cut back. A project that skipped that work has\nnot met criterion 6, however large or transformational it is.\n\n**Phoebe must never offer the latitude as an exemption**, and must never let project scale stand in\nfor the evidence. Where a very large project cannot map every trade-off, the honest answer says what\n*was* assessed and what was left — it does not report the criterion met on the strength of the\nproject's size.\n";

export const WATER_ROUTES_MD: string = "# Routes cards — VWBA 2.0\n\n**For Phoebe (beta) — Eligibility and Feasibility specialist. Pack `vwba-2.0`.**\n\n**Status: approved by the maintainer, 23 Sep 2026.** Graded at the repository root as a draft\nand moved here whole on her word: \"both ship as written; R-8 ships with the Meta line; I confirmed\nthe title.\" Step 4, water half, of the guide-not-gate proposal, her rulings R3 and R6 of\n23 Sep 2026. Nothing reads this file until the routes reader is built (proposal step 5).\n\nNine cards, one cited fix per common gap on the water pathway. **A route is a rewrite, in two or\nthree short sentences, of something the guidebook or one real published project says can be done.**\nNever a fix the engineer thought of. Where no such source exists for a gap, there is no card, and\nPhoebe says so and offers a person instead. All wording is a rewrite; no sentence is copied. The\nsource PDFs stay in `sources-local/`, gitignored, never published.\n\n---\n\n## How a route is used, and what it is not\n\n**A route hangs on a Fixable row.** When Phoebe finds a criterion not met and the card's \"Can it be\nfixed?\" line says *yes* or *depends*, she names the route by its id, and the console renders the\nroute's words with its citation under the row. She never writes a route in her own words that has\nno card. A row with no route card behind it cannot be Fixable; it is Unknown, or the offer of a\nperson.\n\n**A route is a way to try, not a promise.** Every card says so in one line. Taking a route does\nnot make the criterion Met; the evidence the card asks for does.\n\n**Reading level.** Two or three short sentences a twelve-year-old could read, per the rulebook.\nThe card gate prints a grade figure against a grade-6 target and does not enforce it (her rider on\nR8).\n\n---\n\n## Source being cited\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Document** | *Volumetric Water Benefit Accounting 2.0: Guidance for implementing, evaluating, and claiming volumetric water benefits of water stewardship projects* (Guidebook) |\n| **Authors / publisher** | World Resources Institute, LimnoTech, Bluerisk, Bonneville Environmental Foundation. Washington, DC: World Resources Institute |\n| **Version and date** | Version 1, September 2025 |\n| **DOI** | https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112 |\n| **Local copy** | `sources-local/Methodology/vwba-2.0/VWBA_V2.0_Volumetric-Water-Benefit-Accounting-2.0-Guidebook.pdf` — 72 pages; printed page numbers match PDF page numbers 1:1, confirmed against the page footers |\n\n**The one published project cited** (R-8): LimnoTech for Meta, *Volumetric Water Benefits: 2023\nReport*, 25 June 2024, 44 pages; the Navajo Community Water Supply project, pp. 25–26, and\nAppendix 1, p. 39. Local copy `sources-local/Methodology/vwba-2.0/published-reports/Meta_2023_Volumetric_Water_Benefit_Report.pdf`.\nIts\ncanonical address, <https://sustainability.atmeta.com/asset/2023-volumetric-water-benefits/>,\nresolved on 23 Sep 2026; the maintainer confirmed the title at the grade the same day.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-1 — Rework the scope with the implementer\n\n**The gap.** Any criterion not met the first time, on card 1 to card 6.\n\n**The route in plain words.** A project that misses a criterion is not finished. Go back to the\npeople who would build it, talk about what could change in the plan, and check the criteria again.\nThe guidebook draws this loop itself; only a second miss ends in \"not eligible\".\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Step 2.3, Figure 3, \"Flow diagram outlining\nproject selection process\", p. 19.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A reworked scope is checked again from the start; it is not met\nbecause it was reworked.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-2 — Name the shared water challenge from the catchment context\n\n**The gap.** Card 2: the water problem is not yet shown to be real in that place.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Start with the catchment, not the project. Learn its boundaries, its\nsurface water and groundwater, who governs it, and what water problems people there already know\nabout. Desktop research works; talking to the people who depend on the water, especially those hurt\nmost by its problems, works better and may show efforts already under way to join.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Step 1, \"Understand the local catchment\ncontext\", pp. 16–17; Appendix A, criterion 2, \"What a project owner would be asked to show\", p. 32.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** Finding a challenge does not make it the project's; the project's\nobjective still has to be tied to it, which is what card 2 asks for.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-3 — Pick the indicator and the method from the activity table\n\n**The gap.** Card 1: no route to a countable benefit is named yet.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Name the one thing the project is meant to do for the water, then\nlook the activity up in the guidebook's table. The table says what to measure and which method\ncounts it for the common activities. If it is hard to choose, the guidebook says to bring in a\nsubject matter expert.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Step 3, \"Quantify the VWBs of project\nactivities\", and Step 3.1, \"Identify the primary volumetric objective\", p. 21; Appendix C, Table\nC-1, pp. 37–39.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** An activity outside the table is not ruled out, but the table does\nnot count it; a method resting on the same principles has to be named and argued.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-4 — Run a consultation before deciding\n\n**The gap.** Card 3: support from inside the sponsor or from the people around the water is not\nyet shown.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Before the project is chosen, ask the people who depend on that\nwater what they think of it, or gather the desktop evidence that it matters to them. Write down any\nworries that come up, who gains, and what was done with the implementer to keep the trade-offs\nsmall. Then tell the people involved why the project was backed.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix A, criterion 3, \"Internal buy-in and\ngeneral support from external water resources entities\", and its evidence list, p. 33.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** How deep the consultation has to go depends on the place; a risky or\nsensitive place is asked for more, and one round may not be enough.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-5 — Show the work goes beyond a legal duty\n\n**The gap.** Card 4, where the \"Can it be fixed?\" line says *depends*: some legal duty touches the\nwork.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Two doors stay open when a law is involved. If the sponsor must spend\nmoney on environmental work under a wider corporate social responsibility duty, that money may\nstill fund a benefit, as long as the project is additional and meant to be. If the legal duty\nbelongs to the site owner, and that owner cannot or will not do the work, a sponsor who steps in\nmay count the benefit; write down why compliance would not have happened otherwise.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix A, criterion 4, \"Change delivered\nbeyond the without-project conditions\", the two situations outside the bar, and its evidence list,\np. 33.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** Work the sponsor itself must do by law stays out, whatever the\npaperwork says.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-6 — Make a tracking and reporting plan\n\n**The gap.** Card 5: there is no plan yet to keep checking the volumes.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Make the plan before the project starts, with the people who will\nrun it. Decide what will be checked each year: that the work was done, and either the water\nmeasured or the things the number depends on, such as permits, rainfall, structures and upkeep.\nDecide how often and for how long, and put the cost and the people for it in the budget.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix E, \"Making a tracking and reporting\nplan\", pp. 62–64, and Table E-1, p. 64; Step 4.3, \"Make a tracking and reporting plan\", p. 25.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A plan on paper is not capacity; card 5 also asks for evidence the\nmoney and the people exist to run it.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-7 — Look for the downsides before starting\n\n**The gap.** Card 6: nobody has yet looked for the ways the project could hurt someone or backfire.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Before the work begins, look for who might lose: a desktop review,\ntalking to the people affected, or technical evidence, as much as the size and the risk of the place\ncall for. Understand what was found, work with the implementer to make it smaller, and tell the\npeople involved why the project is still worth backing.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix A, criterion 6, \"Trade-offs assessed,\nunderstood, and minimized\", and its evidence list, p. 33.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A very large project gets latitude on how many trade-offs it can\nmap, never on whether it looked.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-8 — Count a drinking-water project as Volume Provided, as a published project did\n\n**The gap.** Card 1 and applies card W2, for a project that gives households or a community access\nto drinking water and does not know how it would be counted.\n\n**The route in plain words.** The guidebook has a method for this: Volume Provided counts the water\na new, restored or improved supply gives people each year, from meters if there are any, else from\nwhat the system can deliver or from how many people get access, how much each uses, and how many\ndays a year. One real project has been counted this way and published: households on the Navajo\nNation given permanent in-home water from tanks and delivery, reported as volume provided.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix D, \"D-3. Volume Provided method\",\npp. 44–45, \"Access to household or community water supply\". LimnoTech for Meta, *Volumetric Water\nBenefits: 2023 Report*, 25 June 2024, \"Navajo Community Water Supply\", pp. 25–26; Appendix 1,\np. 39.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for the\nguidebook. For the published project: <https://sustainability.atmeta.com/asset/2023-volumetric-water-benefits/>,\nMeta's own address for the report, confirmed by the maintainer on 23 Sep 2026. Neither is a mirror\nor a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** The water has to meet local quality standards and the people have to\nhave reasonable access; a supply that fails either is not counted by this method.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-9 — Put the agreement in writing\n\n**The gap.** Card 5, and card 3 where support is real but not recorded.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Once the project is chosen, write an agreement with the people who\nwill build and run it: a contract, a memorandum, a funding agreement. Put in it who does what,\nthe timeline, how and when progress is reported, and how the money flows, including the money for\nreporting and upkeep over the years the benefit is claimed.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Step 4.4, \"Formalize contribution, commitment,\nand support for water stewardship project\", p. 25.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** An agreement records support and a plan; it does not create the\nsupport card 3 asks about.\n";

export const WATER_FEASIBILITY_MD: string = "# Feasibility cards — VWBA 2.0, Appendix B\n\n**For Phoebe (beta) — Eligibility and Feasibility specialist.**\n\n**Status: approved by the maintainer, 21 Aug 2026.**\n\nTen cards, one per selection consideration in Appendix B, none skipped. All wording is a rewrite;\nno sentence is copied. The source PDF stays in `sources-local/`, which is gitignored and never\npublishes — this file cites and links it.\n\n---\n\n## These are not requirements. Read this before any card.\n\n**Every consideration in this file applies only to projects that have already cleared the\neligibility gate.** They help choose well among projects that are already eligible. **None of them\nis a bar to entry, and failing one is not a failure at all.**\n\nThe manual is unambiguous about this. Appendix B opens by placing these considerations on top of\nAppendix A's essential criteria, and says a project needs none of them in order to produce a\nvolumetric water benefit (p. 34). The Step 2.3 summary box says the same from the other direction:\nthey help ensure value and success, but a project does not need them to generate benefits (p. 20).\n\nSo the two sets are different in kind, and Phoebe must never blur them:\n\n| | Eligibility — Appendix A | Feasibility — Appendix B |\n|---|---|---|\n| **What it does** | Decides whether a project can generate a benefit at all | Helps choose among projects that already can |\n| **How it behaves** | Hard gate. Miss one criterion and the project is not eligible | Guidance. Weigh them; none of them excludes |\n| **Number** | Six criteria | Ten considerations |\n| **A shortfall means** | Not eligible, with a route forward | Nothing disqualifying — a factor to weigh |\n\n**Two further points the manual makes, both of which constrain how Phoebe may speak here.**\n\n- **They carry no ranking.** Appendix B states plainly that the considerations are not in priority\n  order, because companies weigh them differently (p. 34). The numbering below is the manual's\n  sequence and nothing more. **Phoebe must not present a lower number as more important.**\n- **Weighing them is the company's job, not Phoebe's.** The manual leaves the relative importance\n  to each company. Phoebe can lay out what a consideration covers and what the manual says to look\n  at; deciding how much it counts for is the user's call.\n\n**How a shortfall should be voiced.** Never *this project fails consideration 4.* Instead: *the\nimplementation timeline is not yet pinned down — here is what the manual suggests looking at, and\nhere is why it may matter to you.* Guidance, offered; never a verdict, delivered.\n\n---\n\n## Source being cited\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Document** | *Volumetric Water Benefit Accounting 2.0: Guidance for implementing, evaluating, and claiming volumetric water benefits of water stewardship projects* (Guidebook) |\n| **Authors / publisher** | World Resources Institute, LimnoTech, Bluerisk, Bonneville Environmental Foundation. Washington, DC: World Resources Institute |\n| **Version and date** | Version 1, September 2025 |\n| **DOI** | https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112 |\n| **Section cited** | Appendix B, \"Project selection considerations\" |\n| **Pages** | 34–36 |\n| **Local copy** | `sources-local/Methodology/vwba-2.0/VWBA_V2.0_Volumetric-Water-Benefit-Accounting-2.0-Guidebook.pdf` — 72 pages; printed page numbers match PDF page numbers 1:1, confirmed against the page footers |\n\nCross-reference used for context: **Step 2.3 summary box, p. 20**, which lists all ten in short form\nand states that they are not required to generate benefits.\n\n---\n\n## How the ten are grouped\n\nThe grouping is the manual's own, stated in the Appendix B introduction on p. 34:\n\n- **Considerations 1–5 — attributes contributing to the likelihood of success.** Will this project\n  actually work, and keep working?\n- **Considerations 6–10 — attributes providing added impact or value.** Beyond working, what else\n  does this project bring?\n\nEach consideration in the source carries three parts, and every card below carries all three: what\nthe consideration covers, why it matters, and how a practitioner evaluates an opportunity against\nit.\n\n---\n\n# Group one — likelihood of success\n\n**Considerations 1 to 5.** Whether the project will work, and go on working.\n\n## Card B-1 — Low risk of the project failing or underdelivering\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** Ask whether the design is sturdy enough to keep producing a\nbenefit as years pass. Look for the ways it could fail or fall short of expectations, and check\nthat something is actually in place to handle the serious ones — including the maintenance and\nrepair that can be seen coming.\n\n**Why it matters.** A project less likely to fail, and one with maintenance and repair arranged,\nstands a better chance of delivering the outputs and impacts hoped for, and lets a practitioner\nclaim benefits with more confidence as time passes. There is a second reason that is easy to miss:\n**if a project does fail and someone else's money is needed to repair it, how the benefit is\ndivided among funders may have to be reopened — and the original claims can shrink.**\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 1 — \"Minimal risk of\nproject failure or underperformance\", p. 34. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- Talk to the people implementing the project to understand the design and the assumptions sitting\n  underneath it, and what they plan to do to keep it going over time.\n- A project without this settled is not disqualified. It is a project where the durability of any\n  claim is less certain, and that is information for the sponsor, not a verdict on the project.\n\n---\n\n## Card B-2 — The implementer is ready and able\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** Two questions about the organisation doing the work. Is it\n*ready* — does it have a route for contracting and receiving money, and are the permits, approvals\nand planning steps both moving and achievable? And is it *able* — does it have the staff,\nknowledge, authorisation, skills, and track record on comparable work? The manual adds one more\nsignal: a track record of working well alongside other practitioners who have backed this kind of\nproject before.\n\n**Why it matters.** An implementer that is not ready, or cannot get the permits and approvals,\nruns into obstacles that stop a project or hold it up.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 2 — \"Project\nimplementer readiness and capacity\", p. 34. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- The manual's own instruction here is brief: work through the traits and conditions above and\n  confirm them.\n- Phoebe should not invent extra tests. The list is the list.\n\n---\n\n## Card B-3 — The money is clear, and so is who claims what\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** Establish what the project costs in total, and what the\nseparate pieces cost — the manual names discovery, design, construction, long-term maintenance and\ntracking — along with what each company is putting in, so it is clear whether the financing is\nactually secured. Weigh the risk of expected money not arriving, and work out who all the parties\nare. Then settle an approach for dividing the benefit among the funders. Ask the implementer\nwhether costs are likely to move later.\n\n**Why it matters.** Where costs and the division of benefit are murky, three things follow:\nunexpected funding gaps, delays, or **the same volume being counted twice without anyone\nintending it.** Getting this clear also shows whether the project could scale if more money\nappeared, and reveals funding dependencies between phases that could affect benefit delivery.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 3 — \"Clarity on project\ncosts and cost shares among funders\", p. 34. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- Get cost information and any funding gaps from the implementer.\n- Ask who the other sponsors are, and work with them on a division of benefit that can be defended.\n- The double-counting point connects to *attribution* in the definitions set — worth reaching for\n  when a project owner asks how shared funding affects what they can say.\n\n---\n\n## Card B-4 — The timeline is known and realistic\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** Work with the implementer so the schedule and its key\nmilestones are both understood and genuinely achievable. This matters most where a company means to\nuse the resulting benefits against goals that have deadlines attached — and the manual notes such\ngoals can involve progress reported along the way as well as at the end.\n\n**Why it matters.** When the schedule and the milestone dates are unclear, delays nobody planned\nfor become more likely.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 4 — \"Feasible project\nimplementation timeline\", p. 34, continuing onto p. 35, where the evaluation guidance finishes.\nShort restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- Get the schedule and milestone detail from the implementer, and form a view of what could cause\n  delay.\n- Keep talking to them regularly, so progress against milestones stays visible rather than being\n  discovered late.\n\n---\n\n## Card B-5 — How long the benefits last, and whether that matches your timeline\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** How long a project keeps delivering varies, and what it\nvaries with is the kind of activity and the way it is funded. The manual contrasts two ends:\nnature-based solutions and infrastructure work usually deliver over a long horizon, while paying\nfor environmental services or changing farming practice may deliver over a much shorter one — it\ngives one to three years as the example. **Neither end is the better answer.** The question is\nwhether the duration a project actually offers lines up with the timeline the sponsor is working\nto.\n\n**Why it matters.** A project still delivering ten years out or more may suit a company with\ndeadlines ahead of it, or one wanting to affect a shared water challenge over a long stretch — but\na shorter-lived project is not thereby worse, only differently matched. Being clear about duration\nup front is described as critical for one specific reason: **it is how the investing company and\nthe implementer avoid misunderstanding each other.** A mismatch discovered late is the failure\nmode this consideration exists to prevent.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 5 — \"Anticipated\nduration of VWBs consistent with desired timeline\", p. 35. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- Ask the implementer how long delivery should last for that activity and that funding structure.\n- Check there is a way to report as often as wanted, right through the period benefits are claimed\n  for.\n- **Allow for the future being uncertain.** The manual asks explicitly that delivery be considered\n  in light of a changing climate and ecosystems that do not stay still — a rare instance of it\n  telling a practitioner to hold a projection loosely.\n\n---\n\n# Group two — added impact or value\n\n**Considerations 6 to 10.** Beyond working, what else the project brings.\n\n## Card B-6 — The location fits the goals being pursued\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** Check that where the project sits actually matches the water\ngoals stated. The manual gives two shapes this can take. A goal may call for a hydrologic link,\ndirect or indirect, to the water a site draws on — or simply for nearness to the site or to the\nlocal community affected. Or a goal may instead call for the project to connect to the company's\nvalue chain, meaning its supply chain or its customers.\n\n**Why it matters.** Water is a local matter, and goals ought to reflect local conditions. A project\nwhose location fits the stated goals is what makes a claim against those goals defensible.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 6 — \"Location relevant\nto water goals\", p. 35. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- A desktop review of what the project looks like, set against the company's water stewardship\n  goals.\n- Note this consideration is about the goals a company has set itself. **It is not a second version\n  of eligibility criterion 2**, which asks whether a real shared water challenge exists in the\n  catchment. A project can sit squarely on a genuine challenge and still be a poor fit for one\n  company's particular goals, and that is a fit question, not a soundness one.\n\n---\n\n## Card B-7 — The project does more than move water\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** Ask whether the project can produce gains beyond volumes of\nwater, and whether it can serve other company goals — the manual lists water quality, water access,\ncarbon, biodiversity, and social or economic impacts. It attaches a practical caveat: reporting\nthose extra gains may mean tracking more than the water alone.\n\n**Why it matters.** A project delivering more than volume addresses a shared water challenge more\ncompletely, and may matter more to the people and bodies who depend on that water. Some companies\nhave set goals reaching past volumetric benefits, and these projects can serve them.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 7 — \"Opportunity to\ndeliver multiple benefits\", p. 35. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- Raise it with the implementer at the start, not once the project is running.\n- The implementer should record the without-project condition, and may have to widen monitoring to\n  capture the additional gains.\n- Describe those gains in words. Where a method exists to put a number on them, use it.\n- **Note the asymmetry.** The volumetric benefit has a prescribed method behind it; these extra\n  benefits may only be describable qualitatively. Phoebe should not let a qualitative account be\n  mistaken for a quantified one.\n\n---\n\n## Card B-8 — The project opens the door to something larger\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** Some projects matter mostly for what they make possible. They\ncan set off action with far more impact than their own, and serve as stepping stones toward\nlarge-scale, transformational work on shared water challenges. They can also create openings to\nshape water governance for the better. The manual points to early-phase work in particular —\nplanning, design, permitting, pilots — the sort that lays groundwork for bigger things to follow.\n\n**Why it matters.** There is both a need and an opening for companies to back large-scale\ntransformational work, and much of it needs early money to clear obstacles and open the way to\nscaling up. The manual adds a second case: **enabling projects can matter most where little other\nwater stewardship work is happening.**\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 8 — \"Enabling\nprojects\", p. 35. Short restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- Ask the implementer whether there is scope here for enabling, repeatable, or scalable work.\n- **A caution Phoebe should carry.** This consideration values a project for what it unlocks\n  later. That is not the same as a countable benefit now — an enabling project must still meet all\n  six eligibility criteria on its own account, including criterion 1's requirement of a countable\n  route. Promise of what may follow does not substitute for that.\n\n---\n\n## Card B-9 — The approach itself breaks new ground\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** Projects that produce benefits *and* try something new — in\nhow they are financed, in the technology used, or in building market systems that can scale — may\nbe ranked higher. On money, the manual points to financing that is sustainable, leveraged, or\ncapable of unlocking further funds, which can open fresh routes to generating benefits, help them\nscale, or raise their impact. On technology, piloting something new can lead to solutions spreading\nthrough the market, or produce an investment structure that suits a wider range of sponsors.\n\n**Why it matters.** There is a need to speed up and unlock chances for companies to set off\nlarge-scale transformational work with significant impact on shared water challenges.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 9 — \"Innovative\nstrategies\", p. 35, continuing onto p. 36, where the reasoning and the evaluation guidance sit.\nShort restatement at p. 20.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- Look at opportunities involving new finance and investment arrangements. The manual's examples\n  are investment funds, microloans, revolving funds, arrangements that channel repayments back into\n  maintaining the project, and projects that improve the policy landscape.\n- Look also at new technologies and at market systems.\n- **This consideration and card B-10 are the two written in the language of priority** — the manual\n  says such projects *may be considered with higher priority* here, and *may be considered to be\n  higher priority* at B-10. In both cases that is a ranking aid among projects already eligible,\n  never a threshold. No other consideration uses priority wording.\n\n---\n\n## Card B-10 — Others are working on it too\n\n**The consideration in plain words.** Projects that produce benefits *and* create room for working\ntogether — through pooled funding and collective action, which the manual glosses as co-designing\nand co-funding — may be ranked higher. Where several corporate funders work alongside on-the-ground\nimplementers, the manual says the value shows up as greater impact, more transparency, and a better\nstory to tell. Collective action can also let one company contribute across a wider set of\nprojects, raising both its engagement and its profile.\n\n**Why it matters.** There is a need to move faster on work that delivers benefits at basin scale.\nThe manual's own phrasing for the rest is blunt: there is strength in numbers, and working together\nproduces more impact at greater scale.\n\n**Source.** VWBA 2.0 (Version 1, September 2025), Appendix B, consideration 10 — \"Opportunity for\ncollaboration\", p. 36. Short restatement at p. 20, where it appears as *Opportunities for\ncollaboration*, plural.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://doi.org/10.46830/wrigb.23.00112> — the publisher's record for this\nguidebook, World Resources Institute. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**How to weigh it — guidance, not a gate.**\n\n- Look at opportunities that involve working with others.\n- The manual goes one step further than evaluation here and suggests joining a regional collective\n  action group, or helping start one, as a way of finding and supporting projects.\n- Where several funders are involved, this consideration runs straight into card B-3's division of\n  benefit. Collaboration adds value and adds the need to settle attribution.\n\n---\n\n# Quick index\n\n| Card | Consideration | Group | Page |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| B-1 | Low risk of failing or underdelivering | Likelihood of success | 34 |\n| B-2 | Implementer is ready and able | Likelihood of success | 34 |\n| B-3 | Costs and cost shares are clear | Likelihood of success | 34 |\n| B-4 | Timeline is known and realistic | Likelihood of success | 34–35 |\n| B-5 | How long benefits last, and whether that matches the timeline | Likelihood of success | 35 |\n| B-6 | Location fits the goals | Added impact or value | 35 |\n| B-7 | Delivers more than water volume | Added impact or value | 35 |\n| B-8 | Opens the door to something larger | Added impact or value | 35 |\n| B-9 | Approach breaks new ground | Added impact or value | 35–36 |\n| B-10 | Others are working on it too | Added impact or value | 36 |\n\n**Count check.** Appendix B holds ten considerations: four beginning on p. 34, five on p. 35, one on\np. 36. Ten cards, none omitted, none invented.\n";

export const CARBON_APPLIES_MD: string = "# Applies cards — Gold Standard safe-drinking-water carbon pathway\n\n**For Phoebe (beta) — Eligibility and Feasibility specialist. Pack `gs-paa-v2.0`.**\n\n**Status: approved by the maintainer, 23 Sep 2026.** Drafted at the repository root on 22 Sep\n2026, its \"If the answer is no\" sections redrafted under the guide-not-gate rule on 23 Sep, graded\n\"passes as redrafted\" with one addition on card T4, and moved here whole on her word. Item K7,\neyeball stop 1. **Nothing reads this file yet**: the pack-keyed reader and gate come at the K7\nproposal's step 5.\n\nFour cards. Together they are the **\"does this apply\" test** for this pack: they say whether\nthe carbon pathway is in play for the project in front of Phoebe. **They never say\neligible.** A pathway that applies still has every worksheet row to meet. All wording is a\nrewrite; no sentence is copied from a source. The source PDFs stay in `sources-local/`,\nwhich is gitignored and never publishes; this file cites and links them.\n\n---\n\n## How the test is run, and what it is not\n\n**Three yes-or-no questions and one sorting question.** Cards T1, T2 and T3 each take a\nyes or a no. Three yeses and the pathway applies: its section of the worksheet opens. A no\nis sorted before anything is ruled out, under the guide-not-gate rule of 23 Sep 2026: each\ncard's \"If the answer is no\" says which kind it is — Fixable, with a cited route; Unknown,\nnot a fail; or Blocked, where no cited route exists — and Phoebe says so once and goes on\nwith whatever other pathway is in play. Card T4 is not yes-or-no; it sets which version of\nthe methodology the project is judged on, and that flag changes what some rows ask for.\n\n**The record first.** What the visitor told Wellington, in \"what it does\" and \"where it is\",\noften settles T1 to T3 without a question. Phoebe asks only for what those words leave\nopen. The \"what kind\" field is a hint from the visitor, never the test's answer.\n\n**Questions cover both pathways where one answer can.** Maintainer's ruling of 22 Sep 2026\n(R4). \"What does the project do, in a sentence?\" answers T1 here and W1 on the water pack\nat once. A verdict covers every card and row the answer settles.\n\n**Not a verdict, not a count.** The four cards carry no state of their own beyond\n*applies* or *does not apply* for the pathway. There is no \"three of four\".\n\n---\n\n## Source being cited\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Document** | *Emission Reductions from Safe Drinking Water Supply* (methodology) |\n| **Publisher** | Gold Standard Foundation, Geneva, under Gold Standard for the Global Goals |\n| **Code** | GS4GG PAA M400-12 |\n| **Version and date** | Version 2.0, 9 July 2026 on the cover and in the document history; every page header and the publisher's page say 7 July 2026. The two-day difference is the publisher's, recorded and not resolved here. In force ninety days after publication (§3.3.1, p. 13). |\n| **Canonical link** | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/ — the publisher's page, which lists version 2.0 and version 1.0 together |\n| **Local copy** | `sources-local/Methodology/V2-PAA/429_V2.0_PAA-M400-12-Emission-reductions-from-Safe-Drinking-Water-Supply.pdf` — 102 printed pages; printed page numbers match the PDF page index |\n\nCard T4 cites two further documents, named on the card, from the Paris-alignment\nframework. No endorsement by Gold Standard is implied anywhere in this file.\n\n---\n\n## Card T1 — Safe drinking water from a technology that gives off little or nothing\n\n**The test in plain words.** The activity puts in a new technology, or brings an existing\none back into service, whose job is to supply drinking water that is safe to drink, and\nthe technology itself gives off no greenhouse gas as it runs, or only a little (for\nexample, a pump on grid electricity). A gravity filter and a handpump are the plain cases\nof zero. Anything that runs on its own fossil-fuel engine is not this.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.1.1 \"Scope\", p. 8; §3.1.4,\np. 9; Table 2, \"Low-emission technology\", p. 7.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** Every project asking about this pathway.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- What the technology is, in a sentence: what it does to the water, or where the water\n  comes from.\n- What powers it, if anything: nothing, sunlight, the grid, or an engine.\n- Whether it is new, or an existing system being restored.\n\n**If the answer is no.** Phoebe sorts the no before she says anything is out. *Blocked* on the\ncarbon pathway if the activity does not supply drinking water at all: the method counts nothing\nelse (§3.1.1, p. 8), no cited route changes that, and the water pathway may still apply, so she\ngoes on with W1. *Fixable* if the only fault is a pump on its own fossil-fuel engine: the method\nnames the technologies that do qualify, handpumps, solar and grid pumps, so the route is a change\nof pump (§3.2.1.1 b and §3.2.1.2, p. 9); the carbon routes card for it is not drafted yet, and she\nsays so. *Unknown* if the visitor cannot say what powers it: she asks, and not knowing is not a\nfail.\n\n---\n\n## Card T2 — Today, people boil, or go without\n\n**The test in plain words.** The people the project will serve get their drinking water\ntoday in one of two ways the method counts. Either they boil unsafe water over wood,\ncharcoal or a fossil fuel, so that safe water from the project would stop that burning.\nOr they drink unsafe water, or too little safe water, because they cannot afford fuel or\nsafe water, or no service reaches them; the method calls this suppressed demand, and it\nis what a small or micro-scale project may count. People who already have safe water from\nan improved source are not a baseline the method counts, unless that source can be shown\nto fail the safe-water definition.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.1.2, p. 8; §3.2.4.1,\np. 10; §7.3.1 \"Baseline scenario\", p. 26; parameter SDWS 12, pp. 52–53.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** Every project asking about this pathway.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- How the people served get drinking water now, in their own words: boiled, and over\n  what; or untreated; or bought; or from a tap.\n- Whether the source they use today counts as improved (a protected well, a borehole, a\n  piped supply) and, if so, whether there is evidence it is unsafe all the same.\n- Roughly how many people, so the scale question later has something to rest on.\n\n**If the answer is no.** *Blocked* on the carbon pathway when the people served already have\nsafe water and nobody boils or goes without: there is no fuel use to stop and no suppressed demand\nto meet, and no cited route makes one (§3.1.2, p. 8; §7.3.1, p. 26). She says that plainly and\ngoes on with the water pathway, which may still apply. *Unknown* when the visitor does not know\nhow people get water today: not a fail. The method's own answer is a baseline survey, and Gold\nStandard publishes the questionnaires for it (§7.3.4, p. 27; SDWS 5, pp. 49–50; workbooks\n429.3–429.12), so she names that route, sets Unknown, and offers the save door.\n\n---\n\n## Card T3 — One of four kinds of technology\n\n**The test in plain words.** The technology is one of the four classes the method knows.\n**Household treatment (HWT):** the water is treated in the home, at the point where it is\ndrunk, by a filter, chlorine, or UV light. **Institutional treatment (IWT):** the same,\non the premises of a school, clinic, prison, or camp. **Community treatment (CWT):** the\nwater is treated at one central point and then piped to homes or collected there, at a\nkiosk or a tap. **Community supply (CWS):** the project makes a supply of water for a\ncommunity, such as a new or restored borehole handpump, a protected spring, or a solar\npump; if that water is not safe on its own, the supply has to be paired with treatment of\none of the other three kinds. Which class it is decides which worksheet rows open: a home\nfilter is never asked about boreholes.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), Table 2 \"Terms and\ndefinitions\", pp. 6–7; Table 3 \"Applicable technologies and calculation methods\", p. 9;\n§3.2.1.1 \"Eligible technologies\", p. 9.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** Every project asking about this pathway. The answer sets the class flag\nthe eligibility rows read: `HWT`, `IWT`, `CWT`, or `CWS`.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Where the water is treated or produced: in each home, at an institution, at one\n  community point, or at a source such as a well or spring.\n- How people get it from there: they treat it themselves, it is piped, or they collect it.\n- For a supply project, whether the water is safe as it comes out, or is treated as well.\n\n**If the answer is no.** *Blocked* on the carbon pathway when the technology is none of the\nfour classes: the method covers nothing else (§3.2.1.1, p. 9; Annex 5 §A5.3.1 c, p. 100), no cited\nroute exists, and she says so and offers to send the project to a person. *Unknown* when the\nvisitor cannot yet say where the water is treated or produced: not a fail. She names the four\nclasses in plain words, asks once, and leaves the row Unknown if they still do not know.\n\n---\n\n## Card T4 — Which version the project is judged on\n\n**The test in plain words.** Two kinds of project reach this pathway, and the method\ntreats them differently in timing, not in substance. **A new project**, not yet\nregistered with Gold Standard, is judged on the 2026 version of the methodology, the one\naligned with the Paris Agreement, because any new project submitted after 30 June 2026\nhas to use a Paris-aligned methodology, and only credits earned under one may be dated\n2026 or later. **A transitioning project**, already certified under the 2021 version,\nkeeps earning credits dated up to the end of 2025 on the old version, but earns nothing\ndated 2026 or later until it has made the move: a revised design and monitoring plan,\nchecked by an approved auditor and reviewed by Gold Standard. The renewal of its\ncrediting period is the point where the move is compulsory. A transitioning project is\njudged on the same worksheet rows as a new one, on a timetable the auditor's requirements\nset for this method: the regulatory-surplus and lock-in rows are re-run at once, before\nany 2026 credit; the water-quality, common-practice and baseline-survey rows are re-run\nat the renewal.\n\n**Source.** *Requirements for Paris Agreement Alignment*, PR100-01 (Version 1.1,\n2 February 2026, effective 1 January 2026), §3.1.1, p. 5; §4.1.1–4.1.3, p. 6;\n§4.3.2–4.3.3, p. 7; §4.4.1, p. 9. *PAA Validation and Verification Requirements for\nMethodologies Update*, VVS100-01 (Version 2.0, 10 July 2026), Annex 5, §A5.1.1–A5.1.4,\np. 44, and Table A5-1, pp. 45–47, the safe-water transition matrix. GS4GG PAA M400-12\n(Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §17.1.1, p. 79, the latest version applies at renewal.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/119_paa_pr_100-01> — the\npublisher's page for the Paris-alignment requirements. Second link, for the transition\nmatrix: <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/120_paa-vvs100-01-paa-validation-and-verification-requirements-for-methodologies-update/>.\nThe 2021 methodology a transitioning project came in on is listed on the methodology's\nown page, cited on cards T1 to T3. None is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** Every project on this pathway. The answer sets the version flag the\neligibility rows read: `new` or `transitioning`.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Whether the project is already registered or certified with Gold Standard, and if so\n  under which version of this methodology.\n- For a transitioning project: whether the PA-alignment design change has been made, is\n  under way, or has not started; and when the current crediting period ends.\n- For a new project: whether it has been submitted for preliminary review, and when.\n\n**If the answer is unknown.** \"I don't know\" is an answer, and it is *Unknown*, not a fail.\nPhoebe treats the project as new, says she has done so, and names the one fact that would change\nit: a Gold Standard registration under the 2021 version. There is a cited route to that fact:\nevery Gold Standard project is public on the Impact Registry from listing onward (Principles &\nRequirements v2.1 §2.2.2, p. 5), so the owner can look the project up by name.\n\n**For a transitioning project, a fact Phoebe phrases.** Maintainer's ruling, 23 Sep 2026: a\ntransition-assistance module, overseen by trusted consultants, is coming to the paid site; the\nvisitor can save the project and sign up for access. Said once, as a fact, where card T4 finds a\ntransitioning project; never a sales line, and never volunteered anywhere else.\n";

export const CARBON_ELIGIBILITY_MD: string = "# Eligibility cards — Gold Standard safe-drinking-water carbon pathway — the methodology's own gate\n\n**For Phoebe (beta) — Eligibility and Feasibility specialist. Pack `gs-paa-v2.0`.**\n\n**Status: approved by the maintainer, 23 Sep 2026** — \"all 17 pass\", with two conditions, both\nmet before the move: the WHO and JMP pages cited on M3, M4, M5 and M7 were confirmed on the\npublishers' sites on 23 Sep 2026 (the scheme report's page dates it 18 March 2026, corrected on\nM3); and the Blocked cases on M5, M12, M13 and M14 go to the reviewer as Q11, a carry on item O14.\nMoved here whole from the root draft. **The fifteen framework and general cards (P1–P2, G1–G13)\nwere approved the same day, \"all pass as drafted\", and are appended below as part two**, so all 32\nrows of the carbon pathway sit in this one file. Items K7, steps 3 and 4. **Nothing reads this file\nyet**: the pack-keyed reader and gate come at the K7 proposal's step 5.\n\n**Every card carries a Phase line**, the maintainer's ruling of 23 Sep 2026: the phase on the\nnavigation where the row is acted on — Eligibility, Partners, Quantify, Plan, Monitor,\nCommunicate — or *To remain eligible*. **Eligibility-phase rows are the free screen**: facts a\nplanner can answer from the idea itself, whose answer can end a pathway or is needed to know it\napplies. On this site Phoebe asks those rows only, and the readiness read is on them. Every other\nrow is shown once, grouped by phase with the seat that helps there, never asked, never a mark\nagainst a planned project; the paid site walks every row with its routes, one gate per phase.\nHere: M1, M2, M5, M6, M9, M13, P1, P2 and G12 are Eligibility; M8, M12, M16, G1–G6, G9 and G10\nPartners; M14 Quantify; M3, M4, M7, M10, M11, M15, G7, G8, G11 and G13 Plan; M17 to remain\neligible, and every *Kept up by* clause, in Monitor.\n\nThirty-two cards, one per basic eligibility requirement, one worksheet row each: seventeen from\nthe methodology itself (part one), fifteen from the framework and the rules for every project\n(part two). Every card carries **Can it be fixed?**, the maintainer's ruling R3 of 23 Sep 2026: it is\nthe only place the word Blocked can come from. All wording is a rewrite; no sentence is copied. The\nsource PDFs stay in `sources-local/`, gitignored, never published; this file cites and links them.\n\n---\n\n## How these seventeen sit with the water cards, and with each other\n\n**The same three kinds of miss.** A row not met today is Fixable, with a cited route; Unknown, with\nwhat to find out; or Blocked, only where the card's line allows. The readiness read for the\ncarbon pathway is drawn from these rows and the fifteen to follow, never from prose.\n\n**Not every row opens for every project.** Each card's **Applies to** names the technology class,\nscale or version flag it binds. A household filter project never hears about boreholes; a\ncommunity supply never hears about the household performance bar. The class comes from applies\ncard T3, the version flag from T4.\n\n**The carbon routes are not drafted yet.** Where a card says a fix exists, it cites the section of\nthe methodology the fix rests on and names the route the proposal planned (its §5.4); the route\ncards themselves come with the second half of step 4. Until then a route named here is a source,\nnot a card Phoebe can render.\n\n**Where an external standard owns the number**, the card says so under **External standard**,\nand sends the reader to it rather than reciting a table (CITATIONS.md, number-dense passages).\n\n---\n\n## Source being cited\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Document** | *Emission Reductions from Safe Drinking Water Supply* (methodology) |\n| **Publisher** | Gold Standard Foundation, Geneva, under Gold Standard for the Global Goals |\n| **Code** | GS4GG PAA M400-12 |\n| **Version and date** | Version 2.0, 9 July 2026 on the cover; the page headers and the publisher's page say 7 July 2026. In force ninety days after publication (§3.3.1, p. 13). |\n| **Canonical link** | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/ — the publisher's page, listing version 2.0 and version 1.0 together |\n| **Local copy** | `sources-local/Methodology/V2-PAA/429_V2.0_PAA-M400-12-Emission-reductions-from-Safe-Drinking-Water-Supply.pdf` — 102 printed pages; printed page numbers match the PDF page index |\n\nWhere a card also cites a framework document — the Principles & Requirements, the Additionality\nRequirements, the Community Services Activity Requirements, the Safeguarding Principles or the\nauditor's transition annex — it names that document, version and page on the card and gives its\npage as a second link. No endorsement by Gold Standard is implied anywhere in this file.\n\n---\n\n## Card M1 — A technology the method names\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The method counts only technologies it names. For treating water:\nceramic, sand, composite or membrane filters; chlorine or bleach dosing; UV light. For supplying\nwater: a new or restored borehole handpump, a protected spring, or a mechanised pump. Anything else\nis outside the method, however well it works.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on one fact: whether the technology can be one the method names.** No\nevidence makes an unlisted technology listed; only a change of technology does. Phoebe asks\nwhether the design could use a listed one; Blocked only when the visitor says it will not change.\nRoute: the list itself (§3.2.1.1, p. 9); planned carbon route R-2 covers the household case.\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.1.1 \"Eligible technologies\",\np. 9; Table 3, p. 9.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** None.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The technology, named the way the method names it.\n- For a supply project, whether the water is safe as it comes out or is treated as well.\n\n---\n\n## Card M2 — No pump on its own fossil-fuel engine\n\n**The rule in plain words.** A pump driven by its own diesel or petrol engine is not eligible. A\nfossil-fuel back-up behind an electric or solar pump is allowed only while it runs for a tenth or\nless of the main technology's operating hours; the hours are logged. Across Gold Standard,\nenergy from fossil fuel is excluded unless a methodology carves it out, and this one carves out\nonly the back-up.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on the pump.** A fossil engine as the main drive is out; the route is\na change of drive to a handpump, solar or grid pump (§3.2.1.1 b, p. 9). A back-up over the tenth\nis fixed by running it less and keeping the log. Blocked only when the visitor says the engine\nstays as the main drive. Planned carbon route: a change of pump, with M1's list as its source.\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility. Kept up by: the back-up engine's hours logged through the crediting period.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.1.2 \"Fossil fuel restrictions\",\np. 9; parameter SDWS 38, pp. 72–73. Also *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025),\n§4.1.7, p. 8.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Second link, for the general rule:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/>. Neither is a mirror or a\nre-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** CWS, and any system with a powered pump or a back-up engine.\n\n**External standard.** None.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- What drives the pump, and whether there is a back-up engine.\n- For a back-up, its running hours against the main technology's, logged.\n\n---\n\n## Card M3 — The treatment technology is shown to work\n\n**The rule in plain words.** A household or institutional treatment technology has to be shown to\nmeet a national standard for household water treatment, or, where the country has none, the WHO\nInternational Scheme's two-star or three-star level, the ones the Scheme calls comprehensive\nprotection. The showing is a lab test report or an official notice; an expert's opinion does not\ncount. The test conditions have to look like the real site, including the water going in.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Get the product's lab report or its listing under the Scheme, or, where\nthe country has a standard, its notice. A technology with no such paper has work to do, not a\nwall; a technology that fails the bar is fixed by choosing one that passes. Route source:\n§3.2.2.1 and SDWS 2; planned carbon route R-2.\n\n**Phase.** Plan.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.2.1 \"Technology performance\",\npp. 9–10; parameter SDWS 2, p. 47.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** HWT, IWT.\n\n**External standard.** The WHO International Scheme to Evaluate Household Water Treatment\nTechnologies sets the star levels: three stars means the product removes at least 4 log of\nbacteria, 5 log of viruses and 4 log of protozoa; two stars at least 2, 3 and 2 (*Results of\nRounds III and IV*, published 18 March 2026, ISBN 978-92-4-011212-4, Table E1, p. ix; §2.2.2, p. 6). A national standard, where one exists,\ncomes first. The figures are the Scheme's; the card sends the reader there.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The maker, the product and the type.\n- The lab report, the Scheme listing, or the national notice.\n\n---\n\n## Card M4 — The water at the collection point is safe\n\n**The rule in plain words.** For a community treatment or supply system, the water where people\ncollect it has to meet two kinds of standard: microbial, by the national standard or, if there is\nnone, the WHO guideline values; and chemical and physical, by the country's priority contaminants\nsuch as arsenic and fluoride, or international guidance where the country has none. It is shown at\nthe start of each crediting period and tested again after anything that could foul the source,\nsuch as a flood. The lab is accredited or approved by the health authority.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Test the water; where it fails, treat it, or pair the supply with a\ntreatment technology, then test again. Route source: §3.2.2.2 and SDWS 3; planned carbon route\nR-3.\n\n**Phase.** Plan. Kept up by: retesting after any event that could foul the source, and at each renewal.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.2.2, p. 10; parameter SDWS 3,\npp. 47–48.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** CWT, CWS.\n\n**External standard.** WHO *Guidelines for Drinking-water Quality*, fourth edition: the microbial\nbar is E. coli not detectable in any 100 ml sample (Table 7.10, 2017 edition p. 149; unchanged in\nthe 2022 and 2026 editions), and the guideline values for arsenic (10 µg/L) and fluoride\n(1.5 mg/L). A national standard, where one exists, comes first.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Test results from an accredited or approved lab, or an approved field kit with its checks.\n- The date of the test against the start of the crediting period.\n\n---\n\n## Card M5 — The people served boil today, or go without\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The method counts two kinds of people. Those who boil unsafe water\ntoday over wood, charcoal or a fossil fuel, so that safe water would stop the burning; and those\nwho drink unsafe water, or too little safe water, because they cannot afford fuel or safe water or\nno service reaches them, which the method calls suppressed demand. People who already have safe\nwater from an improved source are not counted, unless that source is shown to fail the safe-water\ndefinition by tests over six months or literature no older than three years.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on what the people served do today.** Nobody boils and nobody goes\nwithout: Blocked, because there is nothing to count and no route makes it so; the water pathway\nmay still apply. Not known yet: Unknown, not a fail, and the method's own answer is a baseline\nsurvey, with Gold Standard's questionnaires published for it. An improved source suspected unsafe:\nFixable, by testing it. Route source: §7.3.4 and SDWS 5; planned carbon route R-1 (the baseline\nsurvey) and R-4 (showing a source is unsafe).\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.1.2, p. 8; §3.2.4.1, p. 10;\n§7.3.1 \"Baseline scenario\", p. 26; §7.3.4, p. 27; parameters SDWS 5, pp. 49–50, and SDWS 12,\npp. 52–53.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** The WHO/UNICEF JMP definitions of improved and unimproved sources (*Core\nquestions on water, sanitation and hygiene for household surveys*, 2018 update, p. 9), which the\nmethod points at in its Annex 4.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- How the people served get drinking water now, in their own words.\n- Whether the source is improved and, if so, evidence it is unsafe all the same.\n- A baseline survey, or the plan to run one.\n\n---\n\n## Card M6 — Suppressed demand only for small and micro-scale projects\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Only a micro-scale project, up to 10,000 tonnes of CO2e a year, or a\nsmall-scale one, up to 60,000, may count suppressed demand. A large-scale project may count only\npeople shown to be boiling before the project. The general rules say the same: suppressed-demand\nbaselines are for small scale, and a project using one may not stack other products.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on the project's scale.** A large project can still count its\nboilers, or be designed at small scale; a project that stays large and has no boilers has nothing\nto count on this row. Blocked only when the visitor says the project stays large and nobody boils.\nRoute source: §3.2.4.2 and the scale lines of Table 1.\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.4.2, p. 10; Table 1, p. 5. Also\n*Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §4.1.10, p. 9; *Community Services Activity\nRequirements* (v1.2, October 2019), §3.1.3, p. 5; *GHG Emissions Reduction & Sequestration\nProduct Requirements* (v3.2, 12 December 2025), §9.1.5, p. 11.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Second links: <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/>,\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/201-ar-community-services-activity-requirements/>,\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/501-pr-ghg-emissions-reductions-sequestration/>. None is a\nmirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** Every project claiming suppressed demand.\n\n**External standard.** None; the tonnage lines are Gold Standard's.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- A rough estimate of yearly emission reductions against the two scale lines.\n- Whether the people counted boil today, or are counted under suppressed demand.\n\n---\n\n## Card M7 — Suppressed demand comes with basic service\n\n**The rule in plain words.** A project counting suppressed demand has to give its users at least\n\"basic\" drinking-water service on the WHO/UNICEF ladder: an improved source within a thirty-minute\nround trip, queuing included. A household or institutional treatment project treating water from\nan unimproved source qualifies if the water at the point of use reaches basic service; the bar is\nabout the service delivered, not the situation before. An institution already on a public network\nthat supplies safe water cannot count suppressed demand, unless that water is shown to fail the\nsafe-water definition.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Deliver basic service, which is a design choice; for an institution on a\nnetwork, test the network's water over six months or find literature no older than three years.\nRoute source: §3.2.4.3–3.2.4.4 and SDWS 12; planned carbon route R-4.\n\n**Phase.** Plan.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.4.3–3.2.4.4, pp. 10–11; Table 2,\n\"Minimum Service Quality Standard\", p. 7; parameter SDWS 12, pp. 52–53.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** Every project claiming suppressed demand; the network rule for IWT and for\ninstitutions served by a community system.\n\n**External standard.** The WHO/UNICEF JMP service ladder: \"basic\" and \"safely managed\" as defined\nin *Core questions on water, sanitation and hygiene for household surveys*, 2018 update, Table 1,\np. 7.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The service level the design delivers, in the ladder's words.\n- For an institution on a network: the network water's tests or the literature.\n\n---\n\n## Card M8 — Collected water is within reach\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Where people collect water rather than receive it piped, the source\nhas to be within one kilometre of their homes, or a thirty-minute round trip including queuing.\nPeople beyond that line are not counted, unless the project shows, with evidence an auditor\naccepts, that a wider reach fits national water policy for that kind of place, such as arid land or\na dispersed or nomadic population, and that those people really draw their drinking water there.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Count only the people within the line, or gather the policy evidence and\nthe surveys or GPS data for the wider reach. Route source: §3.2.5.1 b; planned carbon route R-5.\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.5 \"Accessibility\", p. 11;\nparameter SDWS 1, pp. 46–47.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** CWT, CWS, where water is collected.\n\n**External standard.** National or regional water-supply policy, for the exception only.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The source's location and the homes it serves, with distances or round-trip times.\n- For a wider reach: the policy it fits, and the surveys or GPS data.\n\n---\n\n## Card M9 — A restored system was really out of action\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Restoring an existing community treatment or supply system counts\nonly if the system had been out of action for at least three months and nobody had a maintenance\nor repair plan for it. The owner shows that with letters from local representatives or government,\nthe original installation date, and what was replaced, when, and by whom.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on the facts of the outage.** The history cannot be changed; the\nevidence for it can be gathered. A system that was working within the last three months, or that\nhad a repair plan, is not a restoration the method counts, and Blocked only when the visitor\nconfirms that. Route source: §3.2.6 and SDWS 2; planned carbon route R-6 for the plan going\nforward.\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.6 \"Rehabilitation\", p. 11;\nTable 2, \"Rehabilitation\", p. 7; parameter SDWS 2, p. 47.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** CWT, CWS, where the system is restored rather than new.\n\n**External standard.** None.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- When the system stopped working, and letters saying so.\n- That no repair plan existed; the original installation date; what was replaced.\n\n---\n\n## Card M10 — The technology's life is known, and replacement is planned\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The technical life of the technology is stated from the maker's\nspecification, a third-party certification or the installer's guarantee, never from an expert's\nopinion alone. Where that life is shorter than the crediting period, the project has a replacement\nor retrofit plan with a performance guarantee; otherwise the claims stop when the technology's life\nends, and units past their life drop out of the count.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Get the specification or guarantee; write the replacement plan, or accept\nclaims that stop at the end of the life. Route source: §3.2.7 and SDWS 7; planned carbon route\nR-7.\n\n**Phase.** Plan. Kept up by: the replacement or retrofit carried out when the technology's life ends.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.7 \"Technical life and\nreplacement\", p. 11; parameter SDWS 7, p. 50; Table 1, p. 5.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** None here; the method also names an A6.4 tool for technical lifetime,\nwhich this repository does not hold.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The technical life, with its source.\n- The replacement or retrofit plan and its guarantee, where the life is shorter than the period.\n\n---\n\n## Card M11 — Nothing is counted twice\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Every unit carries its own identifier in a project database, tracked\nat least to the village or ward. No unit is already in another carbon scheme, and the project does\nnot displace another project's devices. Where a cookstove project serves the same homes, the\nbaseline assumes the efficient stove and the same fuel saving is never claimed twice. Where a\nhousehold project and an institutional project serve the same people, the water credited per\nperson stays under the age cap and the school's share is deducted. Where a jurisdictional forest\nprogramme's reference level counts household fuelwood, the project shows its reductions are not\nissued twice. Across Gold Standard, a project in another programme needs Gold Standard's leave.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Build the database, search the registries, survey the overlap, sign the\ndeclaration; where a forest programme conflicts, get the accounting exclusion or a letter of no\nobjection. Route source: §3.2.8 and SDWS 18–19; planned carbon route R-8.\n\n**Phase.** Plan. Kept up by: the unit database kept current as units are added or replaced.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.8.1 a, b and e, pp. 11–12;\n§3.2.8.2–3.2.8.4, pp. 12–13; parameters SDWS 18 and 19, pp. 55–56. Also *Principles &\nRequirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §3.1.1 c, p. 6.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/>. Neither is a mirror or a\nre-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** Other carbon registries, for the search; the host country's forest\nreference level, where one exists.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- How units are distributed and identified.\n- Registry searches; any overlap survey; the non-overlap declaration where a forest programme exists.\n\n---\n\n## Card M12 — No law already requires it, and the host country has not excluded it\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The reductions must not already be required by an enforced law,\nregulation or funded government scheme. Where a law exists but is not enforced, the project may\nshow that with evidence, except in a high-income country, where every law is treated as enforced.\nAnd the host country must not have put this kind of activity on a list of activities excluded from\ncarbon markets. That is shown in one of three ways: citing the country's official list and finding\nthe activity not on it; a letter of authorisation or no objection from the country's authority; or,\nwhere no list exists, a formal declaration that nothing has been issued excluding it. It is checked\nwhen the project is first assessed, at a transition, and at each renewal.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, and one case ends Blocked.** An enforced law requiring the reductions:\nFixable only by evidence of non-enforcement, and not at all in a high-income country. No list and\nno letter yet: Fixable, by the declaration. The country's list excludes this activity type:\nBlocked, because only the country can change it and no cited route exists. Route source: §6.3.4\nand §6.3.6; planned carbon route R-9.\n\n**Phase.** Partners. Kept up by: re-run at each renewal of the crediting period, and at once for a transitioning project.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §6.3.1–6.3.6 \"Regulatory surplus\" and\n\"Host country eligibility\", pp. 19–21. Also *Requirements for Additionality Demonstration* (v1.0,\n24 April 2025), §5.3.1 a, p. 7, and §6.1.1–6.1.4, p. 11; *PAA Validation and Verification\nRequirements for Methodologies Update* (VVS100-01, v2.0, 10 July 2026), Annex 5, Table A5-1,\n\"Regulatory surplus\", p. 45.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Second links:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/447-requirements-for-additionality-demonstration/>,\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/120_paa-vvs100-01-paa-validation-and-verification-requirements-for-methodologies-update/>.\nNone is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All; a transitioning project re-runs it before any 2026 credit.\n\n**External standard.** The host country's law, and its Article 6 framework, lists and authority.\nOnly a negative-list check is required by the held documents; whether any positive authorisation is\nneeded for a use of the credit is outside them, and the card says nothing beyond the check.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- A summary of the laws and regulations that touch safe-water supply there.\n- The list citation, the letter, or the declaration.\n\n---\n\n## Card M13 — Additional, by the positive list or by analysis\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The project has to show it would not happen without carbon revenue,\none of two ways. Deemed additional, if all four positive-list conditions hold: a listed technology\n(household or institutional filters, chlorination, UV, solar disinfection, or free community\ndispensers); users who lack safely managed water, in a least-developed country or small island\nstate, or in another developing country where the users are below the poverty line and the\ntechnology is below the common-practice line; no full cost recovery from users; and no law\nrequiring it. Otherwise an investment analysis or, for small projects, a barrier analysis. A\nperformance-based route is not allowed. The list is checked at the date of submission and reviewed\nevery three years, and a project that recovers its full cost or is viable without carbon finance\nis off it.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on the money.** Off the list for pricing: Fixable by keeping the\nprice below cost, or by the analysis. Off it for the country or the technology: the analysis.\nFinancially viable without carbon finance, and the visitor says the pricing will not change:\nBlocked, because the standard's own definition of additional is not met. Route source: Annex 5\n§A5.2 and §6.5–6.6; planned carbon routes R-10 and R-11.\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility. Kept up by: the ongoing-financial-need check at each renewal (§6.8, pp. 24–25).\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §6.1.1–6.2.1, p. 19; §6.5–6.6,\npp. 22–23; Annex 5, §A5.2–A5.4, pp. 99–101. Also *Requirements for Additionality Demonstration*\n(v1.0, 24 April 2025), §5.3.2–5.3.6, p. 8; *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025),\n§4.1.46–4.1.47, p. 16; *Community Services Activity Requirements* (v1.2, October 2019), §4.1.9,\np. 6.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Second links:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/447-requirements-for-additionality-demonstration/>,\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/>,\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/201-ar-community-services-activity-requirements/>. None is\na mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** The UN lists of least-developed countries and small island developing\nstates; the host country's national poverty line, or the World Bank's international line for its\nincome class; the World Bank income classification.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Which route: the four list conditions documented, or the analysis.\n- Whether users pay, and whether what they pay covers the full cost.\n\n---\n\n## Card M14 — Not yet common practice\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Every project, on the positive list or not, checks how common the\ntechnology already is. The share of the target market, the people without verifiably safe water\nin the host country or a justified smaller area, already using an equivalent technology has to be\nbelow a quarter, leaving out units paid for by carbon or climate finance. The data can be no older\nthan five years. At or above a quarter, the technology is common practice and the project is not\nadditional. An older rule for community-services projects put the line at a fifth for safe-water\ntechnologies that stop boiling.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on the share and the area.** Above the line nationally but not in a\njustified sub-national area, such as a rural district: Fixable, by the narrower area with its\njustification. At or above the line with no such area: Blocked, because the standard says so and\nno cited route changes the figure. Unknown, when nobody has the data: not a fail; the data is\nwhat to find out. Route source: §6.7.2 c.\n\n**Phase.** Quantify. Kept up by: re-run at each renewal of the crediting period.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §6.7.1–6.7.3 \"Common practice\nanalysis\", pp. 23–24. Also *Requirements for Additionality Demonstration* (v1.0, 24 April 2025),\n§6.5, p. 16; *Community Services Activity Requirements* (v1.2, October 2019), Annex B §1.1.5 and\nits footnote 16, pp. 16–17.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Second links:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/447-requirements-for-additionality-demonstration/>,\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/201-ar-community-services-activity-requirements/>. None is\na mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All; a transitioning project re-runs it at the renewal of its crediting period.\n\n**External standard.** None; the method names Gold Standard's own common-practice tool.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The share of the target market already using an equivalent technology, and the data's date.\n- Where a smaller area is used, why it differs from the country.\n\n---\n\n## Card M15 — No lock-in\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The project must not lock in emissions or technology at odds with the\nhost country's climate strategy. A zero-emission technology, such as a gravity filter, a handpump or\na solar pump, passes outright, whatever its life. A technology with a life under ten years, such as\na ceramic filter, a membrane or UV unit or a chlorine dispenser, passes if it fits the country's\nsustainable-energy strategy, for projects submitted by the end of 2030. A grid-electric system\npasses only with the maker's evidence of a life of ten years or less. Anything else goes through\nthe full lock-in assessment; a fossil back-up within its tenth does not trigger it.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Show the technology's life with the maker's evidence, or state how it\nfits the country's strategy, or run the full assessment. Route source: §6.4.2–6.4.3; planned\ncarbon route R-12.\n\n**Phase.** Plan.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §6.4.1–6.4.4 \"Lock-in risk analysis\",\npp. 21–22. Also *Requirements for Additionality Demonstration* (v1.0, 24 April 2025),\n§6.2.1–6.2.5, pp. 11–12.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/447-requirements-for-additionality-demonstration/>. Neither\nis a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All; a transitioning project re-runs it before any 2026 credit.\n\n**External standard.** The host country's long-term low-emission strategy, where it has one.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- What the technology is and what powers it; its life, with the maker's evidence.\n- Where it is not zero-emission and not short-lived, the assessment.\n\n---\n\n## Card M16 — Groundwater is drawn within what the aquifer can give\n\n**The rule in plain words.** A borehole or other groundwater point that draws more than five cubic\nmetres a day, averaged over the year, needs a hydrogeological assessment, or a permit from the\nnational water authority, confirming the draw is within the aquifer's sustainable yield. At or\nbelow five a day is exempt. The safeguarding principles say the same in general terms: groundwater\nabstraction not above recharge.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Get the assessment or the permit, or design the draw at or below the\nline. Route source: §3.4.7; planned carbon route R-13.\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.4.7 \"Water resource competition\",\np. 14. Also *Safeguarding Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 29 June 2023), Principle 8.2,\npp. 27–28.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/103-par-safeguarding-principles-requirements/>. Neither is\na mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** CWS, where the source is groundwater.\n\n**External standard.** The national water authority, for the permit. The methodology's supplement\nwords the line as \"at or above\" and says grouped handpumps count together; the methodology\ngoverns, so exactly five a day is exempt (Summary, p. 1).\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The expected draw per point, averaged over the year.\n- The assessment or the permit, where the draw is above the line.\n\n---\n\n## Card M17 — The running rules are in the design\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The project documents the regulations that touch safe-water supply,\noperation, maintenance and tariffs there, and does not undermine them. A community treatment or\nsupply system has an ongoing maintenance and repair plan, written into the design, with a log of\noperation, maintenance and downtime kept for monitoring. And the project runs a water-hygiene\neducation campaign every year. The method also asks for the WHO water-safety-plan cycle, safe\nhandling of chemicals, and sound disposal of used filters and containers.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Write the regulatory summary, the maintenance plan and the campaign into\nthe design. Route source: §3.4.2–3.4.3 and §3.2.3.3; planned carbon route R-6.\n\n**Phase.** To remain eligible. For a planned project this row shows in the preview block, never as a mark against it; the plan and the campaign are commitments a planned project makes, and the water pack's consideration B-2, the implementer is ready and able, is the one question that settles it.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.4.2–3.4.3, p. 13; §3.4.4–3.4.6,\npp. 13–14; §3.2.3.3, p. 10; parameters SDWS 4, pp. 48–49, and SDWS 24, pp. 59–60.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All; the maintenance plan and log for CWT and CWS.\n\n**External standard.** The national regulations on water supply and tariffs; the WHO water safety\nplan approach, which the method names without a citation.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The regulatory summary.\n- The maintenance and repair plan; the campaign plan.\n\n---\n\n## Part two — the framework and the rules for every project\n\n**Cards P1–P2 and G1–G13, approved by the maintainer on 23 Sep 2026** (\"all pass as drafted\")\nand appended here whole on her word, after M17, so the carbon pathway's 32 rows sit in one file in\nthe proposal's order: the methodology's own gate first (M1–M17), then the Paris-alignment framework\n(P1–P2), then the rules every Gold Standard project keeps (G1–G13). The same three kinds of miss,\nthe same tags; the routes named are the proposal's planned carbon ids, drafted at the root for her\ngrade.\n\n### Sources cited in part two\n\n| Doc | Document | Version | Canonical page |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 119 | *Requirements for Paris Agreement Alignment*, PR100-01 | v1.1, 2 February 2026, effective 1 January 2026 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/119_paa_pr_100-01 |\n| 120 | *PAA Validation and Verification Requirements for Methodologies Update*, VVS100-01 | v2.0, 10 July 2026 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/120_paa-vvs100-01-paa-validation-and-verification-requirements-for-methodologies-update/ |\n| 201 | *Community Services Activity Requirements* | v1.2, effective 24 October 2019 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/201-ar-community-services-activity-requirements/ |\n| 101 | *Principles & Requirements* | v2.1, 31 January 2025, effective 5 December 2024 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/ |\n| 103 | *Safeguarding Principles & Requirements* | v2.1, 29 June 2023 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/103-par-safeguarding-principles-requirements/ |\n| 102 | *Stakeholder Consultation and Engagement Requirements* | v2.1, 14 June 2022 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/102-par-stakeholder-consultation-requirements/ |\n| 104 | *Gender Equality Requirements & Guidelines* | v2.0, 16 May 2023 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/104-par-gender-equality-requirements-and-guidelines/ |\n| 118 | *Requirements for Selection of Monitoring Indicators in the SDG Impact Tool* | v1.0, 18 October 2025 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/118_par_requirements-for-monitoring-indicator-selection/ |\n| 501 | *GHG Emissions Reduction & Sequestration Product Requirements* | v3.2, 12 December 2025 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/501-pr-ghg-emissions-reductions-sequestration/ |\n| 429 | *Emission Reductions from Safe Drinking Water Supply*, GS4GG PAA M400-12 | V2.0, 9 July 2026 | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/ |\n\nEvery page resolved on 22 or 23 Sep 2026 and named the version held. Publisher: Gold Standard\nFoundation, Geneva. No endorsement is implied. Local copies sit under\n`sources-local/Methodology/`, in `PAA-framework/`, `mandatory-for-all-projects/`, `V1-legacy/`\nand `V2-PAA/`.\n\n---\n\n## Card P1 — The right version of the method\n\n**The rule in plain words.** A new project submitted to Gold Standard after 30 June 2026 uses the\nParis-aligned version of the method, the 2026 one. A project already certified under the 2021\nversion keeps earning credits dated up to the end of 2025 on it, but earns nothing dated 2026 or\nlater until it has made the move: a revised design and monitoring plan, an approved auditor's\ncheck, and Gold Standard's review. The renewal of its crediting period is where the move becomes\ncompulsory. A transitioning project is judged on the same rows as a new one, on the auditor's\ntimetable for this method: regulatory surplus and lock-in re-run at once, common practice, water\nquality and the baseline survey re-run at renewal.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on whether the move will be made.** A new project: the right version\nis simply the one it uses. A transitioning project that will make the design change: Fixable, the\nchange itself is the route. One that says it will not: Blocked for any credit dated 2026 or later,\nby the standard's cut-off, with no route around it. Route source: 119 §4.3.2; planned carbon route\nR-19.\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility. Kept up by: for a transitioning project, the re-runs Annex 5 sets at renewal.\n\n**Source.** *Requirements for Paris Agreement Alignment*, PR100-01 (v1.1, 2 February 2026),\n§3.1.1, p. 5; §4.1.1–4.1.3, p. 6; §4.3.2, p. 7; §4.4.1, p. 9. *PAA Validation and Verification\nRequirements for Methodologies Update*, VVS100-01 (v2.0, 10 July 2026), Annex 5, §A5.1.1–A5.1.4,\np. 44; Table A5-1, pp. 45–47.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/119_paa_pr_100-01> — the publisher's\npage for the Paris-alignment requirements. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/120_paa-vvs100-01-paa-validation-and-verification-requirements-for-methodologies-update/>.\nNeither is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All; the second half only a transitioning project (applies card T4's flag).\n\n**External standard.** None.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Whether the project is registered with Gold Standard and under which version.\n- For a transitioning project: whether the design change is done, under way, or not started.\n\n---\n\n## Card P2 — A community-services activity\n\n**The rule in plain words.** This method sits under Gold Standard's community-services rules, so\nthe project has to be one: it provides or improves access to a service at household, community or\ninstitution level, and water and sanitation is one of the named services. Treating industrial\nwastewater is not a community service and is outside these rules.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** A safe-water project for households, a community or an institution is a\ncommunity-services activity by definition; the card exists so the family is named and its rules\nknown. A project that turns out to be industrial wastewater treatment has already failed applies\ncard T1, which is where that is said.\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility.\n\n**Source.** *Community Services Activity Requirements* (v1.2, October 2019), §2.1.2, p. 3 and its\nfootnote 1; §3.1.1 d, p. 4. Named as binding by GS4GG PAA M400-12 (V2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.4.1,\np. 13.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/201-ar-community-services-activity-requirements/>\n— the publisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>.\nNeither is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** None.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Who is served: households, a community, or an institution.\n\n---\n\n## Card G1 — A real activity on the ground, with a boundary\n\n**The rule in plain words.** A Gold Standard project involves physical action on the ground, and\nit has a defined project area and boundary, drawn on a map.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Draw the boundary; describe the sites. A safe-water project is physical\nby nature.\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §3.1.1 a and c, p. 6.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** None.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- A boundary map and a description of the sites.\n\n---\n\n## Card G2 — The project keeps the host country's law\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The project complies with the host country's legal, environmental,\necological and social rules. Where the safeguarding rules are stricter than the law, the stricter\none applies.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Get the permits; meet the rules. Unknown, when the owner has not yet\nlooked: what to find out is which rules touch safe-water supply there, which M17's regulatory\nsummary also asks.\n\n**Phase.** Partners. Kept up by: staying within the rules while the project runs.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §3.1.1 d, p. 6. *Safeguarding\nPrinciples & Requirements* (v2.1, 29 June 2023), §4.1.2, p. 8.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/103-par-safeguarding-principles-requirements/>. Neither is a\nmirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** The host country's law.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Permits, and evidence of compliance.\n\n---\n\n## Card G3 — Everyone involved is named and in good standing\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Every participating organisation is named with its contacts, is\nlegally registered and allowed to operate, and is in good standing: no insolvency, and no legal or\ncriminal notices against it or its directors. Gold Standard may refuse a project on reputational\ngrounds.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Register; resolve what is outstanding; supply the documents. Unknown,\nwhen a partner's standing is not yet known: what to find out is the partner's registration and\nany notices.\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §3.1.1 e, p. 6.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** The jurisdiction's company registry.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Each organisation's registration certificate and a good-standing document.\n\n---\n\n## Card G4 — Ownership of the credits is clear and agreed\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Whoever claims the credits has full, uncontested ownership of them.\nWhere the rights pass from the people who use the devices, the transfer is transparent and made\nwith their free, prior and informed consent; for a distributed project the ownership along the\nchain is described, told to every participant, maker and retailer in writing, and discussed at\nthe local consultation. Any dispute over tenure or rights is reported to Gold Standard at once.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Write the ownership statement and the consent forms; put the assertions\nin the contracts. Route source: V2.0 §3.2.8.1 c–d; planned carbon route R-8.\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §3.1.1 f, p. 7. *Community\nServices Activity Requirements* (v1.2, October 2019), §3.1.4, p. 5. GS4GG PAA M400-12 (V2.0,\n9 July 2026), §3.2.8.1 c–d, p. 12; parameters SDWS 18 and 20, pp. 55–57.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Second links:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/201-ar-community-services-activity-requirements/>,\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>.\nNone is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** None; free, prior and informed consent is the standard's own term.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The ownership and transfer agreements; the end-users' consent records.\n\n---\n\n## Card G5 — The other rights the project rests on are not in dispute\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Rights the project depends on, such as water rights and rights of\naccess, are uncontested where the rules require them. A dispute is declared at once and settled\nbefore work continues in the place it affects.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Settle the dispute, or get the right; work pauses there until it is\nsettled. Unknown, when the owner has not yet checked who holds the water right: what to find out.\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §3.1.1 g, p. 7.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All; a community supply drawing water first.\n\n**External standard.** The host country's law on water rights.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The rights and permits; any dispute and how it was settled.\n\n---\n\n## Card G6 — Aid money is declared, and none of it buys the credits\n\n**The rule in plain words.** In a country on the OECD's list of aid recipients, any official\ndevelopment assistance behind the project is declared to Gold Standard at design certification.\nA project is not eligible if that assistance is given on condition that the credits go to the\ndonor country.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on the condition.** Aid with no strings on the credits: Fixable by the\ndeclaration. Aid given on condition that the credits go to the donor: Blocked unless the condition\nis removed, which is the donor's to change; the card says so and leaves it Unknown until the\nowner has asked.\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §3.1.1 h, p. 7. *GHG Emissions\nReduction & Sequestration Product Requirements* (v3.2, 12 December 2025), §6.1.1–6.1.2, p. 6.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/501-pr-ghg-emissions-reductions-sequestration/>. Neither is\na mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All, in a country on the OECD DAC list of aid recipients.\n\n**External standard.** The OECD DAC list of ODA recipients.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Whether any aid money is behind the project, and on what terms; the signed declaration.\n\n---\n\n## Card G7 — Three Sustainable Development Goals, one of them climate\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The project shows a real, direct and intended contribution to at\nleast three Sustainable Development Goals, and one of them is SDG 13, climate action. A safe-water\nproject reaches SDG 13 and SDG 6 by design; the third is the owner's to name. The contribution has\nto come from the project itself, not from its construction or a one-off, and it is monitored by\nindicators chosen from Gold Standard's own tool.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Name the third goal and its indicator. Route source: 101 §4.1.12–4.1.17.\n\n**Phase.** Plan. Kept up by: the chosen indicators monitored and reported through the crediting\nperiod.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §4.1.12–4.1.13, pp. 9–10;\n§4.1.16–4.1.17, p. 10. *Requirements for Selection of Monitoring Indicators in the SDG Impact\nTool* (v1.0, 18 October 2025), §3.1.1–3.1.2, p. 3. GS4GG PAA M400-12 (V2.0, 9 July 2026),\n§3.2.4.5, p. 11.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Second links:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/118_par_requirements-for-monitoring-indicator-selection/>,\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>.\nNone is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** The UN and national SDG indicator sets, as the standard's options allow.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The three goals and, for each, the contribution and its indicator.\n\n---\n\n## Card G8 — The safeguarding assessment is done and answered\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Before certification the project completes Gold Standard's\nsafeguarding assessment against every principle, answers every question, writes mitigation for\neach identified risk into its design, and carries the \"yes\" and \"potentially\" answers into its\nmonitoring. For a water project the principles that bite hardest are community health and safety,\nwater resources, Indigenous peoples and land tenure. Some answers need an expert stakeholder's\nreview.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Complete the assessment; write the mitigation; appoint the expert where\na principle calls for one. Route source: 103 Annex 1; planned carbon route R-16.\n\n**Phase.** Plan. Kept up by: the assessment's open answers reported in each monitoring report.\n\n**Source.** *Safeguarding Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 29 June 2023), §4.1.1, p. 8; §5.1.1\nand Table 3, pp. 10–11; Annex 1, pp. 40–41; Principle 3, pp. 14–15; Principles 4.3–4.4,\npp. 17–20; Principle 8, pp. 27–28. *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025),\n§4.1.19–4.1.23, p. 11.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/103-par-safeguarding-principles-requirements/>\n— the publisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/>. Neither is a mirror or a\nre-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** The principles cite the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and the UN\ntreaties, the ILO conventions, and the UNDP social and environmental standards; the card sends the\nreader to the principles for each.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The completed assessment with its justifications, and the mitigation plan.\n\n---\n\n## Card G9 — The people affected were consulted, in two rounds, before the start\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Stakeholders are consulted before the project's start date, in at\nleast two rounds: a physical meeting, open to anyone, invited at least thirty days ahead with\ninformation shared a week before; and a feedback round of at least thirty days afterwards, with\nthe updated documents public. The invitation list has a minimum: the people affected, holders of\nland rights, local and national officials, local groups and women's groups, Gold Standard and its\nsupporters in the country. A project that started first is \"retroactive\": it explains why, and\nholds its meeting before it is submitted for preliminary review.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Hold the rounds. For a running project, hold the meeting before\nsubmission and explain how comments will be taken into account. Route source: 102 §3.4–3.6;\nplanned carbon route R-14.\n\n**Phase.** Partners.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §4.1.26–4.1.28, p. 12; §4.1.42,\np. 15. *Stakeholder Consultation and Engagement Requirements* (v2.1, 14 June 2022), §3.1.3,\n§3.2.1–3.2.2, pp. 6; §3.3.1, §3.4.2, §3.5.2, pp. 6–8; §3.6.1, §3.6.6–3.6.7, pp. 8–9.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/102-par-stakeholder-consultation-requirements/>. Neither is\na mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** None.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- Meeting records, invitations with their dates, and the consultation report.\n\n---\n\n## Card G10 — A way to raise a grievance, for the life of the project\n\n**The rule in plain words.** A formal way for anyone to give input or raise a grievance exists for\nthe whole life of the project. Its method is agreed at the physical meeting; at minimum a grievance\nbook sits at an agreed place; every comment is acknowledged in writing unless it was anonymous; and\nwhat came in and what was done is reported in each annual or monitoring report.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Set it up and agree it at the meeting. Route source: 102 §3.8; planned\ncarbon route R-15.\n\n**Phase.** Partners. Kept up by: the book kept, comments acknowledged and reported through the\ncrediting period.\n\n**Source.** *Stakeholder Consultation and Engagement Requirements* (v2.1, 14 June 2022),\n§3.8.1–3.8.6, pp. 10–11; §3.10.1–3.10.2, p. 11. *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January\n2025), §4.1.34, p. 13.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/102-par-stakeholder-consultation-requirements/>\n— the publisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/>. Neither is a mirror or a\nre-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** None.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The mechanism as described in the consultation report; later, its log.\n\n---\n\n## Card G11 — Gender-sensitive by design\n\n**The rule in plain words.** Every Gold Standard project is gender-sensitive: its design is\nchecked against the host country's gender policy and Gold Standard's gender principles; it meets\nthe gender safeguarding principle, doing no harm to women's work burden, access to resources or\nownership; and its consultation was run so that women could take part equally. Going further,\nto claim gender goals, is optional.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Take the three steps; a gender expert is recommended and may be\nrequired. Route source: 104 §2.1; planned carbon route R-17.\n\n**Phase.** Plan.\n\n**Source.** *Gender Equality Requirements & Guidelines* (v2.0, 16 May 2023), §1.1.1, p. 5;\n§2.1.1–2.1.7, pp. 5–6. *Stakeholder Consultation and Engagement Requirements* (v2.1, 14 June\n2022), §3.1.2, pp. 5–6. *Safeguarding Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 29 June 2023), Principle\n2, pp. 13–14.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/104-par-gender-equality-requirements-and-guidelines/>\n— the publisher's page. Second links:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/102-par-stakeholder-consultation-requirements/>,\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/103-par-safeguarding-principles-requirements/>. None is a\nmirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** The host country's national gender strategy.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The gender context in the design; the consultation records showing equal participation.\n\n---\n\n## Card G12 — Submitted within a year of starting\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The project is submitted to Gold Standard for preliminary review\nwithin one year of its start date. The start date is the first committed spend on\nimplementation, not land or feasibility work; for distributed devices, the day the first unit is\nput to use. A project whose consultation came after its start is retroactive, and a retroactive\nproject submitted later than a year after its start is not eligible. Credits reach back at most\ntwo years before design certification.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Depends, on the calendar.** Within the year: Fixable, submit now. Past the\nyear, for a retroactive project: Blocked, by the standard's own words, with no route that reopens\nthe year; Phoebe says to look at the water pathway. A planned project has not started the clock.\nRoute source: 101 §4.1.49; planned carbon route R-18.\n\n**Phase.** Eligibility.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §4.1.39–4.1.42, pp. 14–15;\n§4.1.49, p. 17; §5.1.37, p. 27. GS4GG PAA M400-12 (V2.0, 9 July 2026), Table 1, \"start date\",\np. 5.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>.\nNeither is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** None.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The first committed spend or the first installation, dated, against the submission date.\n\n---\n\n## Card G13 — A plan to monitor and report\n\n**The rule in plain words.** The design carries a monitoring and reporting plan that names, for\neverything tracked, the measure, how often, how and by whom it is collected, and its quality\ncontrol. For this method that means the usage surveys, the water-quality tests, the distribution\nor flow records, and a database of units; the plan is presented at the auditor's check and is what\neach monitoring report follows.\n\n**Can it be fixed? Yes.** Write the plan into the design; the method's own annex says what it\nholds. Route source: 101 §4.1.43–4.1.45; V2.0 §14.1; the water pack's route R-6 is the same idea\non the other pathway.\n\n**Phase.** Plan. Kept up by: running it, which for a planned project\nshows in the preview block and is settled by consideration B-2.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §4.1.43–4.1.45, pp. 15–16. GS4GG\nPAA M400-12 (V2.0, 9 July 2026), §14.1.1–14.1.3, pp. 45–46.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>.\nNeither is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**Applies to.** All.\n\n**External standard.** None here; the method also names the CDM sampling standard for surveys,\nwhich this repository does not hold.\n\n**What a project owner would be asked to show.**\n\n- The plan, with its measures, frequencies, collectors and checks.\n";

export const CARBON_ROUTES_MD: string = "# Routes cards — Gold Standard safe-drinking-water carbon pathway\n\n**For Phoebe (beta) — Eligibility and Feasibility specialist. Pack `gs-paa-v2.0`.**\n\n**Status: approved by the maintainer, 23 Sep 2026.** Graded at the repository root as a draft\nand moved here whole on her word. Step 4, carbon half, of the guide-not-gate proposal; her word\nof 23 Sep 2026: cited only from the methodology, the documents it names as binding, or a held\npublished project. Nothing reads this file until the routes reader is built (proposal step 5).\n\nNineteen cards, one cited fix per common gap on the carbon pathway, the ids the eligibility cards\nalready name. **A route is a rewrite, in two or three short sentences, of something the source\nsays can be done.** Never a fix the engineer thought of. No held published project is a carbon\nproject, so no card here cites one; the gaps with no source in the held documents have no card,\nand Phoebe says so and offers a person instead. All wording is a rewrite; no sentence is copied.\nThe source PDFs stay in `sources-local/`, gitignored, never published.\n\n---\n\n## How a route is used, and what it is not\n\n**A route hangs on a Fixable row.** When Phoebe finds a row not met and the card's \"Can it be\nfixed?\" line says *yes* or *depends*, she names the route by its id, and the console renders the\nroute's words with its citation under the row. A row with no route card behind it cannot be\nFixable; it is Unknown, or the offer of a person.\n\n**A route is a way to try, not a promise.** Every card says so in one line. Taking a route does\nnot make the row Met; the evidence the row asks for does.\n\n**Which documents may be cited here, and how.** The methodology itself; the documents it names as\nbinding, at §3.4.1 (the Principles & Requirements, the Safeguarding Principles, the Community\nServices Activity Requirements) and at §4.1.2 (the additionality, baseline and leakage\nrequirements, its tools, the product requirements); and, where the Principles & Requirements in\nturn name a requirement as binding on every project, that document, with the chain shown on the\ncard. Four cards rest on that second link (R-14, R-15, R-17, R-19); the grader notes name them so\nshe can strike any she reads as too far from the methodology.\n\n**Reading level.** Two or three short sentences a twelve-year-old could read. The card gate prints\na grade figure against a grade-6 target and does not enforce it.\n\n---\n\n## Source being cited\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Document** | *Emission Reductions from Safe Drinking Water Supply* (methodology) |\n| **Publisher** | Gold Standard Foundation, Geneva, under Gold Standard for the Global Goals |\n| **Code** | GS4GG PAA M400-12 |\n| **Version and date** | Version 2.0, 9 July 2026 on the cover; the page headers and the publisher's page say 7 July 2026 |\n| **Canonical link** | https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/ |\n| **Local copy** | `sources-local/Methodology/V2-PAA/429_V2.0_PAA-M400-12-Emission-reductions-from-Safe-Drinking-Water-Supply.pdf` — 102 printed pages; printed page numbers match the PDF page index |\n\n**The documents it names as binding, cited on the cards below**, each with the page confirmed on\n22 or 23 Sep 2026: *Principles & Requirements* v2.1 (101), *Safeguarding Principles &\nRequirements* v2.1 (103), *Community Services Activity Requirements* v1.2 (201), *Requirements\nfor Additionality Demonstration* v1.0 (447), *Requirements for Paris Agreement Alignment* v1.1\n(119, named at §6.3.6 through the PA-Alignment Design Change), and, one link further through 101,\n*Stakeholder Consultation and Engagement Requirements* v2.1 (102) and *Gender Equality\nRequirements & Guidelines* v2.0 (104). Their canonical pages are in the sources table of part two\nof `eligibility-cards-gs.md`. No endorsement by Gold Standard is implied anywhere in this file.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-1 — Design the baseline survey\n\n**The gap.** M5 and applies card T2, when nobody yet knows how the people served get their water\ntoday.\n\n**The route in plain words.** The method says how to find out: a baseline survey of the people\nthe project will serve, asking what they drink, whether they boil, over what fuel, and how much.\nThe method's own annex says how the survey is designed and run, and Gold Standard publishes the\nquestionnaire workbooks for it. Run it before the design is fixed, and the baseline row answers\nitself.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §7.3.4, p. 27; parameters SDWS 5, 6 and\n8, pp. 49–51; Annex 1, §A1.1, p. 83. The questionnaire workbooks 429.3 to 429.12 are Gold\nStandard's own, held locally under `sources-local/Methodology/survey-templates/`.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology, which also lists the survey workbooks. Not a mirror\nor a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** The survey may find that nobody boils and nobody goes without, and\nthen the carbon pathway has nothing to count.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-2 — Get the technology's performance shown\n\n**The gap.** M3, and M1 where the technology is a household or institutional treatment unit.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Ask the maker for the lab test report, or the product's listing\nunder the WHO scheme, or the national notice where the country has a standard. If the product\nhas none, choose one that does: the scheme publishes which products reached two or three stars.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.2.1, pp. 9–10; parameter SDWS 2,\np. 47; §4.1.2.4 c, p. 15, naming the WHO scheme as a source the method relies on.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A listed product still has to be tested under conditions that look\nlike the site; an expert's opinion does not count.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-3 — Test the water at the collection point\n\n**The gap.** M4.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Take samples where people collect the water and have them tested\nfor the microbial and the chemical standards, at an accredited lab or with an approved field kit\nwhose checks are documented. If the water fails, treat it or pair the supply with treatment, then\ntest again.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.2.2, p. 10; parameter SDWS 3,\npp. 47–48; §14.4.3, p. 76, on field kits.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** One passing test starts the crediting period; the water is tested\nagain after any event that could foul the source, and every year at the point of use.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-4 — Show the source people use today is unsafe\n\n**The gap.** M5 and M7, where the people served already draw from an improved source, or an\ninstitution sits on a public network.\n\n**The route in plain words.** An improved source is not counted as unsafe on anyone's word. Test\nit over six months, or find published evidence no older than three years that it fails the\nsafe-water definition. With that in hand, the people who use it count.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), parameter SDWS 12, pp. 52–53;\n§3.2.4.4, p. 11.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A source that tests safe stays out of the count, whatever people\nbelieve about it.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-5 — Claim the wider reach with evidence\n\n**The gap.** M8, where homes lie beyond a kilometre or a thirty-minute round trip.\n\n**The route in plain words.** The method allows a wider reach in places where the usual line does\nnot fit, such as arid land or a scattered or nomadic population. Show that the arrangement fits\nthe country's or region's water policy, and show with surveys or GPS data that those people really\ndraw their drinking water at the source. The auditor checks it.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.5.1 b, p. 11; parameter SDWS 1,\npp. 46–47.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** Without the policy fit and the usage evidence, only the people\nwithin the line count.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-6 — Write the maintenance plan and keep the log\n\n**The gap.** M17, and M9 for a restored system going forward.\n\n**The route in plain words.** For a community system, write down who maintains it, how often,\nand what happens when it breaks, and put that in the design. Then keep a log of operation,\nmaintenance and downtime; the monitoring reads from it. Add the yearly water-hygiene campaign to\nthe same plan.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.4.3, p. 13; parameter SDWS 31;\n§3.2.3.3, p. 10, and parameter SDWS 24, pp. 59–60, for the campaign.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A plan is a commitment; days of downtime in the log are days not\ncredited.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-7 — Plan the replacement\n\n**The gap.** M10, where the technology's life is shorter than the crediting period.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Get the technology's life from the maker's specification, a\ncertification or the installer's guarantee. If it is shorter than the years you mean to claim,\nwrite a replacement or retrofit plan with a performance guarantee. Otherwise, plan to claim only\nuntil the life ends.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.7, p. 11; parameter SDWS 7, p. 50;\nparameter SDWS 33, p. 68.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** Units past their life drop out of the count whether or not a plan\nwas written.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-8 — Give every unit an identifier and get the consents\n\n**The gap.** M11 and G4.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Give each unit its own identifier in a project database, tracked at\nleast to the village or ward. Put the ownership of the credits in writing with every partner,\nmaker and retailer, and get each user's informed consent that the credits are not theirs to claim.\nSearch the other carbon registries so no unit is counted twice.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.2.8.1, pp. 11–12; parameters SDWS 18,\n19 and 20, pp. 55–57; §14.1.3, pp. 45–46. *Community Services Activity Requirements* (v1.2,\nOctober 2019), §3.1.4, p. 5, named as binding at §3.4.1.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/201-ar-community-services-activity-requirements/>. Neither\nis a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** Where a cookstove project or a forest programme overlaps, the\nmethod's overlap rules still have to be worked through.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-9 — Check the host country's list, or declare there is none\n\n**The gap.** M12, the host-country half.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Find the country's official list of activities it excludes from\ncarbon markets, published by its climate authority, and cite it if the activity is not on it. Or\nget a letter of authorisation or no objection from that authority. Where no list exists, make a\nformal declaration that nothing excluding the activity has been issued.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §6.3.5–6.3.6, pp. 20–21.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A list that names the activity as excluded ends the pathway; no\ndeclaration overrides it.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-10 — Meet the four positive-list conditions\n\n**The gap.** M13.\n\n**The route in plain words.** A project can be counted as additional without a financial\nanalysis if it meets four conditions and documents them: a listed technology; users without safely\nmanaged water, in a least-developed country or small island state, or elsewhere below the poverty\nline with the technology still uncommon; no full cost recovery from users; and no law requiring\nit. Write each one into the design document with its evidence.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §6.2.1 a, p. 19; Annex 5, §A5.2.1–A5.2.4,\npp. 99–100; §A5.4.1, p. 100.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** The list is checked at the date of submission and reviewed every\nthree years; common practice, regulatory surplus and lock-in still have to be shown.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-11 — Keep the price below cost\n\n**The gap.** M13, where the project would recover its full cost from users.\n\n**The route in plain words.** The positive list turns on money: the users' payments must not\ncover the full delivered cost, so that carbon finance is what bridges the gap. Give the technology\nfree, or sell it at a subsidised price below cost, and document the gap. A project that would pay\nfor itself from users is not additional under the list.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), Annex 5, §A5.2.3, p. 100; §A5.3.1 a,\np. 100.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** Below-cost pricing is a design choice with a cost of its own; it\ndoes not make the other three conditions true.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-12 — Show the technology's life is under ten years\n\n**The gap.** M15, for a grid-electric or other technology that is not zero-emission.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Get the maker's specification showing the specific model's\ntechnical or operational life is ten years or less, and say how the project fits the country's\nsustainable-energy strategy. With those, the lock-in check is passed for projects submitted by the\nend of 2030. A zero-emission technology needs nothing here.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §6.4.2–6.4.3, pp. 21–22.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A technology with a longer life, or a project submitted after 2030,\ngoes through the full lock-in assessment instead.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-13 — Get the abstraction assessed or permitted\n\n**The gap.** M16.\n\n**The route in plain words.** If a borehole will draw more than five cubic metres a day on\naverage, get a hydrogeological assessment, or a permit from the national water authority, showing\nthe draw sits within what the aquifer can sustain. Or design the draw at or below the line, where\nno assessment is needed.\n\n**Source.** GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §3.4.7, p. 14.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>\n— the publisher's page for this methodology. Not a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** The safeguarding principle on water still applies whatever the\nfigure: abstraction not above recharge.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-14 — Hold the two consultation rounds\n\n**The gap.** G9.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Invite everyone the rules name, at least thirty days ahead, and\nshare a plain summary of the project a week before. Hold a physical meeting open to anyone; then\nrun a feedback round of at least thirty days with the updated documents public. For a project\nthat has already started, hold the meeting before submitting and say why it came late.\n\n**Source.** *Stakeholder Consultation and Engagement Requirements* (v2.1, 14 June 2022),\n§3.3.1, §3.4.2, §3.5.2, pp. 6–8; §3.6.1, §3.6.6–3.6.7, pp. 8–9. The chain: the methodology names\nthe *Principles & Requirements* as binding (§3.4.1, p. 13), and they require consultation per\nthat document for every project (101 v2.1, §4.1.24–4.1.28, p. 12).\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/102-par-stakeholder-consultation-requirements/>\n— the publisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/>. Neither is a mirror or a\nre-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A late consultation does not reopen the one-year submission window\nof G12.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-15 — Set up the grievance book\n\n**The gap.** G10.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Agree at the physical meeting how anyone can raise a concern, and at\nthe least keep a grievance book at a place the community agreed on. Acknowledge every comment in\nwriting and report what came in and what was done with it in each annual or monitoring report.\n\n**Source.** *Stakeholder Consultation and Engagement Requirements* (v2.1, 14 June 2022),\n§3.8.1–3.8.6, pp. 10–11; §3.10.1–3.10.2, p. 11. The chain as on R-14: 101 v2.1, §4.1.34, p. 13,\nnamed as binding by the methodology at §3.4.1.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/102-par-stakeholder-consultation-requirements/>\n— the publisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/>. Neither is a mirror or a\nre-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** The book has to be kept for the life of the project, not only set\nup.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-16 — Answer the safeguarding checklist\n\n**The gap.** G8.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Work through Gold Standard's safeguarding checklist, every\nquestion, before certification. Where an answer is \"yes\" or \"potentially\", write what the project\nwill do about it into the design, and plan to report on it. Where a principle calls for an expert\nstakeholder, appoint one and give Gold Standard their no-conflict statement.\n\n**Source.** *Safeguarding Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 29 June 2023), §4.1.1, p. 8;\n§5.1.1 and Table 3, pp. 10–11; Annex 1, pp. 40–41; §4.2.1, pp. 9–10. Named as binding by the\nmethodology at §3.4.1, p. 13.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/103-par-safeguarding-principles-requirements/>\n— the publisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>.\nNeither is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** A \"no\" needs a justification too; an unanswered question is a\nnon-conformity.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-17 — Take the three gender steps\n\n**The gap.** G11.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Check the design against the country's gender policy and Gold\nStandard's gender principles; answer the gender safeguarding principle; and run the consultation\nso that women can take part equally. A gender expert's input is recommended and may be asked for.\n\n**Source.** *Gender Equality Requirements & Guidelines* (v2.0, 16 May 2023), §2.1.1–2.1.7,\npp. 5–6. The chain: the consultation requirements make gender-sensitive certification mandatory\nfor every project (102 v2.1, §3.1.2, pp. 5–6), and the *Principles & Requirements* bind the\nconsultation requirements (101 v2.1, §4.1.24, p. 12), named by the methodology at §3.4.1.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/104-par-gender-equality-requirements-and-guidelines/>\n— the publisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/102-par-stakeholder-consultation-requirements/>. Neither is\na mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** The optional gender-responsive route, for claiming gender goals,\nis more than this and is not this card.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-18 — Submit within the year\n\n**The gap.** G12, while the year is still open.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Count one year from the first committed spend, or from the first\nunit put to use. Before that day, submit the project for preliminary review: the key project\ninformation, a draft consultation report, the signed terms and the fee. Listing, not\ncertification, follows; but the clock is stopped.\n\n**Source.** *Principles & Requirements* (v2.1, 31 January 2025), §4.1.39–4.1.41, pp. 14–15;\n§4.1.49, p. 17; §5.1.5 and §5.1.7–5.1.8, pp. 21–22. Named as binding by the methodology at\n§3.4.1, p. 13.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/101-par-principles-requirements/> — the\npublisher's page. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/429-ee-sws-emission-reductions-from-safe-drinking-water-supply/>.\nNeither is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** Past the year, no submission reopens it.\n\n---\n\n## Route R-19 — Make the Paris-alignment design change\n\n**The gap.** P1, for a project certified under the 2021 version that wants credits dated 2026 or\nlater.\n\n**The route in plain words.** Revise the design document and the monitoring plan to the 2026\nversion of the method, have an approved auditor check the change, and put it through Gold\nStandard's review. The auditor's own requirements say what is re-checked now and what waits for\nthe renewal of the crediting period.\n\n**Source.** *Requirements for Paris Agreement Alignment*, PR100-01 (v1.1, 2 February 2026),\n§4.3.2–4.3.3, p. 7. GS4GG PAA M400-12 (Version 2.0, 9 July 2026), §6.3.6, p. 20, which names the\nPA-Alignment Design Change, and §17.1.1, p. 79. *PAA Validation and Verification Requirements for\nMethodologies Update*, VVS100-01 (v2.0, 10 July 2026), Annex 5, Table A5-1, pp. 45–47.\n\n**Canonical link.** <https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/119_paa_pr_100-01> — the publisher's\npage. Second link:\n<https://globalgoals.goldstandard.org/120_paa-vvs100-01-paa-validation-and-verification-requirements-for-methodologies-update/>.\nNeither is a mirror or a re-hosted copy.\n\n**What it does not promise.** Credits dated up to the end of 2025 keep the old rules; nothing\nafter that issues until the change is approved.\n";
